Reliable probability ratio estimates are critical for the robustness of our sensitivity study performed in~\cref{sec:results}. To assess the accuracy of the trained NNs in performing this task, we carry out a series of diagnostic tests, following approaches similar to those used by recent work by the ATLAS~collaboration~in~\cite{ATLAS:2024jry,ATLAS:2024ynn}. All evaluations are performed on datasets that are statistically independent from those used in training and validation.

For the probability ratio in~(\ref{eq:carl}), we compare the classifier decision function outputs, $\hat{s}(x)$, with the fraction of events originating from the numerator hypothesis among those sampled from the balanced hypothesis, as shown in~\cref{fig:carl_calibration}. For a well-calibrated classifier, the calibration curve appears as a diagonal line in the upper panel of the plot. The observed NSBI results closely follow this trend, indicating good calibration within statistical uncertainties. A corresponding calibration closure test for the $pp \to ZZ \to 2\ell 2\nu$ training is presented in~Appendix~\ref{sec:llnunuDiagnostics}.

