\section{Non-universality of the type of the transition}
\label{sec: transition}



The nature of the transition exhibits significant variability depending on the specific details of the microscopic interactions. For instance, one could introduce energy during collisions through a velocity-dependent coefficient of restitution $\alpha(v)$ instead of adding an extra velocity term $\Delta$:

\begin{equation}
    \begin{split}
        \bm v_i'&= \bm v_i + \dfrac{1+\alpha(\bm v_{ij})}{2}(\bm v_{ij}\cdot \hat{\bm\sigma}_{ij})\hat{\bm\sigma}_{ij} \\
        \bm v_j'&= \bm v_j - \dfrac{1+\alpha(\bm v_{ij})}{2}(\bm v_{ij}\cdot \hat{\bm\sigma}_{ij})\hat{\bm\sigma}_{ij} ,
    \end{split}
\end{equation}

with $\alpha(\bm v_{ij}) = \alpha_0e^{-|\bm v_{ij}|/v_0}$ and $\alpha_0 > 1$. At low relative velocity the collision adds energy into the system ($\alpha > 1$) while at large relative velocities it drains it ($\alpha < 1$). The resulting energy transfer of a collision is thus qualitatively similar as the one found with a $\Delta$ and a constant $\alpha$. However, this model leads to a discontinuous transition. Indeed, the rate of change of the temperature $G$ (see Eq.~\eqref{eq: G}) of this model is:
%
\begin{widetext}
\begin{equation}
        G = \dfrac{\omega}{2}\left[\alpha_0^2m\sqrt{a/18\pi}\left(\dfrac{\sqrt{\pi/2}(4/3 a^2+6ab^2+b^4)e^{2b^2/a}\mathrm{erfc}(b/\sqrt{a/2})}{a^{7/2}} \right.\right. \\ -\left.\left.\dfrac{5ab + b^3}{a^3}\right)-T\right]-2\gamma T,
\end{equation}
\end{widetext}
%
with $a = m/2T$ and $b = 2/v_0$. One can numerically see that imposing $G = 0$ leads to a discontinuous transition.

Another example of dependence on the microscopic description would be the type of drag. Using dry friction with the simple $\Delta$ model:
\begin{equation}
    \dfrac{\mathrm{d} \bm v_i}{\mathrm{d} t} = -\tilde \gamma \dfrac{\bm v_i}{|\bm v_i|},
\end{equation}
also leads to a discontinuous transition.
These observations on alternative models provide additional evidence for the appropriateness of the coarse-grained model introduced in the main text. Indeed, for the synchronization dependent $\Delta$ model, we are sure that the occurrence of the discontinuous transition is entirely given by the synchronization mechanism as empirically verified with the realistic simulations.
