\begin{comment}
\section{Correction to the collision frequency}
\label{sec: collisionFrequency}

To recover a phase transition theoretically when there is no synchronization, we need to account for correction to the Enksog's frequency of collision at densities close to the critical packing fraction where the damping of the velocity with time as a particle free fly is important. 

As with the derivation of the value of the critical point, we start by considering the mean free path of a particle, which is the average distance it travels between collisions. Assuming that a particle after a collision has velocity $v_0$ and travel a time $\tau_f$ before the next collision then:

\begin{equation}
    l(\phi) = \dfrac{v_0}{\gamma}\left(1 - e^{-\gamma \tau_f}\right).
\end{equation}

By definition, $\tau_f$ is the travel time of the particle, hence its inverse corresponds to the frequency at which the particle collide with an other one. We can express $\tau_f$ as:

\begin{equation}
    \tau_f = \dfrac{-1}{\gamma}\ln\left(1 - \gamma l(\phi)/v_0\right).
    \label{eq: tau}
\end{equation}

Imposing $\tau_f \rightarrow \infty$ and $v_0 = \Delta$ (the minimum possible post collision velocity), we recover $\phi_c$ from the mean free path argument. To go further than that, we have to define a temperature $T$. For example by taking it as the time average of the kinetic energy of one particle:

\begin{equation}
    \begin{split}
        T &= \dfrac{1}{2}m\dfrac{1}{\tau_f}\int_0^{\tau_f}|v^2(t)|\mathrm{d}t\\
        &=\dfrac{mv_0^2}{4}\dfrac{(1 - \gamma l(\phi)/v_0)^2 - 1}{\ln (1 - \gamma l(\phi)/v_0)}.
    \end{split}
    \label{eq:T}
\end{equation}

Solving numerically this equation allows us to determine $v_0(\phi, T)$ such that at packing fraction $\phi$ the system has a temperature $T$. 

It is important to note that both Eq.~\eqref{eq:T} and \eqref{eq: tau} do not use any form of ensemble averaging which is necessary to get back the equilibrium limit. We can introduce a correction factor $\sqrt{\pi}/2$ to the term $v_0$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq: tau} to simulate a gaussian average of (initial) velocities so that in the limit $\gamma\rightarrow 0$ we recover: 
\begin{equation}
    \lim _{\gamma\rightarrow 0}1/{\tilde \tau_f} = \omega_{e}(\phi, T) = \dfrac{\langle \sqrt{v_0^2} \rangle_{MB}}{l(\phi)},
\end{equation}
 where $\langle ...\rangle_{MB}$ denotes an average with a Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution. The correction factor arises from the fact that $\sqrt{\langle v^2\rangle_{MB}} = \sqrt{\pi/4}\langle \sqrt{v^2}\rangle_{MB}$. As discussed in Sec.~\ref{sec: cont vs discont}, this assumption breaks down in the vicinity of the transition due to the existence of a population of inactive particles.

Eq. (\eqref{eq: tau}) allows us to define a frequency of collision:
%
\begin{equation}
    \omega(\phi, T) = \dfrac{1}{\tilde\tau_f(\phi, v_o(\phi, T))} = \dfrac{-\gamma}{\ln\left(1-2\gamma l(\phi)/(v_0\sqrt{\pi}) \right)}.
\end{equation}
%
It is worth noting that as expected from the mean free path argument when the mean free path exceeds a certain threshold, the collision frequency ceases to exist. This occurs because particles do not possess sufficient initial velocity to reach their neighboring particles within the given timescale, as dictated by the parameter $\gamma$. 

Unfortunately, while this theory correctly predicts the first decay of the temperature at the transition, it also predicts a discontinuous transition due to two main factors. Firstly, we introduced an \textit{ad hoc} prefactor  $\sqrt{\pi}/2$ in front of the exit velocity to emulate a gaussian average. However, this approximation introduces an inconsistency between the temperature and the frequency of collision since we find that $\tilde T = T(v_0 = 2\gamma l/\sqrt{\pi})>0$ while $\omega(\phi, \tilde T) = 0$. Moreover, close to the transition, the exit velocity distribution is not gaussian but sharply peaked in $\Delta$. Secondly, molecular chaos is not expected to hold close to the transition. To recover a continuous transition, albeit with a logarithmic non analyticity instead of the expected power law, we can work with the balance of energy purely from the exiting velocity. To illustrate our point, we neglect the dissipative nature of the collisions and suppose that collisions simply add an energy $m\Delta^2$ into the system as in Ref.~\onlinecite{lei2019hydrodynamics}, where a continuous transition is also found. This assumption holds in our system close to the transition point. In this case, the steady state is governed by:
\begin{equation}
    m\Delta^2 = mv_0^2(1 - e^{-2\gamma\tau_f}).
    \label{eq: balance}
\end{equation}
 Using Eqs.~\eqref{eq: tau}, \eqref{eq:T} and \eqref{eq: balance}, the temperature close to the critical packing fraction is immediately given by:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
    T &= \dfrac{-m\Delta^2/4}{\log\left(\dfrac{l( \phi_c)^2 - l(\phi)^2}{l(\phi_c)^2 + l(\phi)^2}\right)}\\&=\dfrac{-m\Delta^2/4}{\log\left(\dfrac{l'(\phi_c)(\phi_c -\phi)}{l(\phi_c)}\right)} + \mathcal{O}\left(\dfrac{\phi_c-\phi}{\log\left(\dfrac{l'(\phi_c)(\phi_c -\phi)}{l(\phi_c)}\right)^2}\right)
\end{split}
\end{equation}

with $l(\phi_c) =  \Delta/\gamma$.  This construction embeds the mean free path prediction of the critical packing fraction and predicts a (wrong) exponent of 0 since the divergence is logarithmic. It is important to note that this argument is primarily pedagogical. Obtaining a reasonable estimate of the temperature close to the critical packing fraction with this method would necessitate a non-trivial ensemble average over exiting velocities and positions, which proves challenging due to the non-gaussian behavior of the velocity distribution, the strong breakdown of molecular chaos and the presence of hyperuniformity.
\end{comment}

\section{Tricritical point and continuous transition with synchronization}
\label{sec: appendix tricritical}


\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.99\columnwidth]{Figures/potential+discontinuous_app.pdf}
    \caption{a) Transition from the discontinuous to continuous transition with synchronization as $\alpha$ is decreased: $N = 10000$, $\hat \tau\Delta/\sigma = 0.025$, $\hat\tau/\tau_s=0.25$ and $\hat \tau\gamma = 0.01$. b) Is a close-up on the transition for $\alpha = 0.8$ with a scaling analysis showing that the last active steady state $\Delta T_{ss}$ goes to 0 with system size, proving the continuity of the transition. The dashed line is the expected power law with the exponent from the conserved directed percolation universality class.}
    \label{fig: tricritical}
\end{figure}

As discussed in the main text, even with synchronization, we can observe a continuous transition because the synchronization time competes with a finite mean collision time in the active region. Thus, at a large but finite synchronization time $\tau_s$, significantly longer than the minimum mean collision time in active regions, synchronization will not influence the transition, which remains continuous. A less straightforward example of a continuous transition at finite $\tau_s$ occurs when $\alpha \ll 1$ as illustrated by Fig.~\ref{fig: tricritical}a. Indeed, we observe that by decreasing $\alpha$, the transition becomes continuous. The continuity of the transition is made explicit in Fig.~\ref{fig: tricritical}b where a scale analysis is performed for $\alpha = 0.8$ and shows that the temperature of the last active steady state tends to approach 0 with increasing system size. While the theory cannot predict quantitatively this transition, it still gives a qualitative answer. As evidenced from Eq.~\eqref{eq: K52}, in the case of small $\alpha$, a discontinuous transition is theoretically predicted to occur at a small $T_a$. However, the population of inactive particles will reach 0 before the theoretical discontinuous transition happens, leading to a continuous transition instead. Similar transitions have been investigated in sandpiles belonging to the conserved directed percolation universality class \cite{di2016self}. In those systems, the mean field approximation has been shown to quantitatively fail considerably around the passage from the discontinuous to continuous transition, notably, it has been found that the continuous transition for a range of parameters, is fluctuation induced.

This behavior implies the existence of a non-equilibrium tricritical point in our system. Such a phenomenon has been documented and thoroughly investigated in a closely related system in Ref.~\onlinecite{lei2021barrier}. It would be interesting to observe a tricritical point in the realistic system as well. It will require further exploration, for example through the use of very inelastic beads such as plastic ones. Note that a tricritical point as been observed as well for the liquid-solid phase transition of a quasi-2D granular gas \cite{guzman2018critical, castillo2012fluctuations, castillo2015universality}.




\section{Expression for the transport coefficients and numerical values}
\label{sec: appendix transport}

As mentioned in the main text, we use the simple Enskog's equilibrium transport coefficients. They should give reasonable results in the active state. Moreover, while our simulations are mostly done at $\phi = 0.1$, the theory works well at higher densities (see Fig.~\ref{fig: adiabatic}, where $\phi = 0.5$ for instance). Here, we provide a list of the expressions used. They are derived assuming molecular chaos and a Gaussian velocity distribution. We will also use the equilibrium contact value of the pair correlation function \cite{mulero2009equation}:
%
\begin{equation}
    \chi(\phi) = \dfrac{1 - 7\phi/16 - \phi^3/20}{(1-\phi)^2}.
\end{equation}
%
The pressure is given by \cite{garzo2018enskog}:
%
\begin{equation}
    p = \rho T(1 + (1+\alpha)\phi \chi(\phi))+8 \sqrt{\pi m}\phi\rho \chi(\phi)\Delta\sqrt{T}.
\end{equation}
%
The transport coefficients we used are the equilibrium ones from Enskog's theory \cite{gradenigo2011fluctuating}:
%
\begin{equation}
    \begin{split}
     \eta &= \frac{1}{2\sigma}\sqrt{\frac{mT}{\pi}}\left(\chi^{-1} + 
   2\phi + (1 + 8/\pi)\chi \phi^2\right)\\
   \zeta &= \dfrac{8\phi^2}{\sigma\pi}\chi\sqrt{\frac{mT}{\pi}}\\
   \kappa &= \dfrac{2}{\sigma}\sqrt{\frac{T}{m\pi}}\left(\chi^{-1} + 3\phi + (9/4 + 4/\pi)\chi\phi^2\right)\\
   \mu &= 0\\
   \nu &= 0
    \end{split}
\end{equation}
%
For the transport coefficients taking into account $\Delta$ and $\alpha$, see Ref.~\onlinecite{garzo2018enskog}. The transport coefficient with $\Delta+\gamma$ are not known and should be derived from the Chapman-Enskog procedure.

