\label{eq: charactistic polynomial M}
\end{equation}
\end{widetext}

Even at equilibrium ($\gamma = 0$, $G_T =0$ and $G_\rho = 0$), this is a third order polynomial, which is complicated to handle. A simple approximated solution is provided by expanding $z(k)$ through an asymptotic power expansion in $k$ solved order by order:

\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
    k&\to k \varepsilon ,\\
    z(k) &= z^{(0)} + z^{(1)}\varepsilon + z^{(2)}\varepsilon^2 + \dots.
\end{split}
\label{eq: simple stupid expansion}
\end{equation}

The hydrodynamic theory being valid only at small $k$ renders this approximation very suitable since we do not need to know the solutions at larger $k$, where the mathematical theory breaks down anyway. Proceeding with the expansion defined by Eqs.~\eqref{eq: simple stupid expansion} is simple because the roots at $k = 0$ are trivial. This perturbation series at second order gives the eigenvalues:
