\section{Stability of the homogeneous state}
\label{sec: appendix stability}

Here we address the question of the stability of our system at every finite $k$ and prove rigorously the stability criterion Eq.~\eqref{eq: stability criterium}.

The eigenvalues are given by the roots of the characteristic polynomial Eq.~\eqref{eq: charactistic polynomial M}. The Routh–Hurwitz stability criterion states that a polynomial:
\begin{equation}
    a_3z^3+a_2z^2+a_1z + a_0 = 0
\end{equation}
has only negative roots if $a_{0, 1, 2, 3}>0$ and $a_2a_1-a_3a_0>0$. With our characteristic polynomial $a_3>0$. Moreover, since $G_T<0$, $a_2>0$ and $a_1>0$. The first non trivial conditions arise with $a_0$ and $a_2a_1-a_3a_0$ which can be computed from Eq.~\eqref{eq: charactistic polynomial M} and read respectively:

\begin{equation}
    \begin{split}
    a_1>0\Rightarrow & -\text{det}[\bm M(k)]>0,\\
    a_2a_1-a_3a_0>0\Rightarrow &-\text{det}[\bm M(k)]+F(k)>0,
    \end{split}
    \label{eq: stability criterion finite k}
\end{equation}
with $F(k)$ a complicated function verifying $0<F(0)<F(k>0)$. 

Therefore, in our case, the system is unstable if:
\begin{equation}
    \text{det}[\bm M(k)] < 0,
\end{equation}
with:
\begin{equation}
    \begin{split}
    \frac{\text{det}[\bm M(k)]}{-\gamma - \eta_0^{\perp}k^2/\rho_0} =& \left(\left.G_Tp_\rho\right|_{\rho_0, T_0}-\left.G_\rho p_T\right|_{\rho_0, T_0}\right)k^2 + \\
    &  \left(\dfrac{\mu_0}{n_0}\left.p_T\right|_{\rho_0, T_0}-\dfrac{\kappa_0}{n_0}\left.p_\rho\right|_{\rho_0, T_0}\right)k^4.
    \end{split}
    \label{eq: determinant}
\end{equation}

the term in $\bm k^4$ is beyond the domain of validity of thermodynamics, therefore, we focus on:
\begin{equation}
   \left.G_T\right|_{\rho_0, T_0} \left.p_\rho\right|_{\rho_0, T_0} > \left.G_\rho\right|_{\rho_0, T_0}\left.p_T\right|_{\rho_0, T_0}.
   \label{eq: stability criterium}
\end{equation}
This criterion is numerically never verified in our system without synchronization or with the effective $\Delta$. Thus, it is not causing an instability.

