\begin{document}
\title{The Geometric P=W conjecture \\
and Thurston's compactification}
\author{Ashwin Ayilliath-Kutteri, Mohammad Farajzadeh-Tehrani, Charles Frohman}
\date{\today}
\maketitle



\begin{abstract}
In this paper, we derive an explicit formula for a well-known embedding of the set of isotopy classes of multicurves on a closed surface of genus $g$ into $\mathbb{N}^{9g - 9}$. More significantly, we relate this result and established facts about Thurston's compactification of Teichm\"uller space to the geometric P = W conjecture for $\tn{SL}(2,\C)$, which concerns projective compactifications of character varieties of closed surfaces. In particular, we construct a projective compactification of the $\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb{C})$-character variety for any closed surface of genus $g > 1$, in which the boundary divisors are toric orbifolds and their dual intersection complex is a sphere.\end{abstract}
\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction and the main results}

In this section, we review the $9g - 9$ Theorem, the P=W conjecture, and their connection. Our main result in the first part is an explicit formula for intersection numbers with a set of $9g-9$ curves in terms of Dehn-Thurston coordinates. Then we prove a result about the behavior of these intersection numbers under the product map in the skein algebra. As a corollary, we obtain a result that is stronger than the prediction of the geometric P=W conjecture for $\mathrm{SL}(2, \mathbb{C})$-character varieties. \\

In a nutshell, Thurston’s compactification of Teichmüller space adds a sphere at infinity of dimension $6g - 5$, which is the base of a real cone identified with the space of measured foliations -- real-weighted analogs of multicurves on a surface. While this cone naturally lives in an infinite-dimensional space, it is often embedded into a finite-dimensional vector space, where it becomes a rational polyhedral cone over a piecewise-linear (PL) sphere. The combinatorics of this PL sphere determine a collection of convex polyhedra, each giving rise to a projective toric variety of twice the real dimension, and these varieties intersect along toric strata dictated by the structure of the polyhedral complex. We will identify this resulting toric complex with the boundary of a projective compactification of the character variety, thereby relating the sphere envisioned in the geometric P=W conjecture and Thurston's boundary of Teichm\"uller space in a strong sense.



\subsection{Preliminaries and the $(9g-9)$ Theorem}
Suppose $\Sigma$ is a closed, oriented surface of genus $g$ with $g > 1$. 
Let $\mathcal{S}$ denote the set of isotopy classes of non-trivial\footnote{i.e., curves that do not bound a disk.} simple closed curves (embedded circles) on $\Sigma$. 
For $c_1, c_2 \in \mathcal{S}$, let $i(c_1, c_2)$ denote their geometric intersection number, defined as the minimum number of intersection points of a simple curve $c'_1$ isotopic to $c_1$ with a simple curve $c'_2$ isotopic to $c_2$, where $c'_1$ is transverse to $c'_2$. 
More generally, let $\mathcal{S}'$ denote the set of isotopy classes of simple, closed, but not necessarily connected curves on $\Sigma$, called {\bf multicurves}, whose components all belong to $\mathcal{S}$. 
The definition of geometric intersection naturally extends to a symmetric pairing on $\mathcal{S}'$.\\


Geometric intersection with elements of $\mathcal{S}$ induces a map
\bEq{iota_mc} 
\iota \colon \mathcal{S}' \lra \N^{\mathcal{S}} \subset \R_{\geq 0}^\cS
\eEq 
which is known to be injective. 
Closely related to multicurves, there exists a space\footnote{Denoted by $\cM\cF$ in \cite{FLP}.} $\mathcal{F}$ of (Whitehead equivalence classes of) {\bf measured foliations} on $\Sigma$ admitting a similar embedding
\bEq{iota_F} 
\iota \colon \cF \lra  \R_{\geq 0}^\cS
\eEq
whose image is a real cone in $\R_{\geq 0}^\cS$ homeomorphic to $\R^{6g-6}$. There is a natural inclusion $\cS'\subset \cF$ such that (\ref{iota_mc}) is the restriction to $\cS'$ of (\ref{iota_F}). In other words, the elements of $\cF$ should be thought of as multicurves with positive real weights.\\

We will denote the projectivization of these embeddings, taking values in
$$
\mathbb{P}(\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^\cS) := \big( \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^\cS \setminus \{0\} \big) / \mathbb{R}_+,
$$
by $\iota_\mathbb{P}$. It is well known (cf.~\cite[Ch.~1]{FLP}) that:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\iota(\cF)$ is a real closed cone over $\iota_\mathbb{P}(\cF) \cong S^{6g-5} \subset \mathbb{P}(\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^\cS)$.
\item Moreover,
\begin{equation} \label{closure}
\overline{\iota_\mathbb{P}(\cS)} = \overline{\iota_\mathbb{P}(\cS')} = \iota_\mathbb{P}(\cF).
\end{equation}
\end{itemize}
The original context for  these results is the construction of a compactification of Teichmüller space. In this paper, we only use the fact that the image of $\cF$ under such an embedding is a cone over a sphere.
 \\

{\it Since $\cS$ is an infinite set, it is an interesting problem to find finite subsets $\cC \subset \cS$ that yield embeddings with similar properties.} \\

We review some known and expected answers here.  A {\bf pants decomposition} of $\Si$ is a collection 
$\cP =\{a_1, . . . , a_{3g-3}\}$ of disjoint non-trivial curves such that no two of them are isotopic. The collection $\cP$ cuts $\Si$ into $2g-2$ pairs of pants (i.e., three-holed spheres). 
Given a pants decomposition $\mathcal{P}$, a {\bf dual graph} $\Gamma$ to $\mathcal{P}$ is a connected trivalent graph embedded\footnote{Abstractly, the dual graph is unique, though there are different ways to embed it into $\Sigma$.} in $\Sigma$ with exactly $2g-2$ vertices -- one in each pair of pants -- and $3g-3$ edges ${e_1, \dots, e_{3g-3}}$, where each edge $e_i$ intersects $a_i$ transversally at a single point and is disjoint from $a_j$ for $j \neq i$.\\

Given $(\cP, \Gamma)$ as above, the {\bf Dehn–Thurston (DT)} coordinate function
\begin{equation} \label{DTembedding}
\cS' \lra \mathbb{N}^\cP \times \mathbb{Z}^\cP \subset \mathbb{Z}^{6g - 6}
\end{equation}
assigns to each multicurve its intersection numbers with the curves in $\cP$, along with certain twist parameters; see Section~\ref{sec:DT-coordinates}. The definition of the twist parameters depends on the choice of $\Gamma$.\\

While \eqref{DTembedding} is an embedding and there is a straightforward recipe for drawing standard representatives of each multicurve based on its Dehn–Thurston coordinates, the fact that the twist coordinates can take negative values makes them unsuitable for the applications discussed later in this paper. Also, they are not easily related to the coordinates arising from the embeddings~\eqref{iota_mc}, and they do not behave well under the product of multicurves in the skein algebra of the surface. We will get around the last issue by using the modified DT coordinates recently introduced in \cite{BKL}; c.f. Section~\ref{sec:modDT}.  \\


If we seek coordinate functions selected from a finite sub-collection of $\cS$, the multicurve version of the famous $(9g - 9)$ Theorem asserts that $\cS'$ (respectively, $\cF$) can be embedded into $\mathbb{N}^{9g - 9}$ (respectively, $\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{9g - 9}$) using intersection numbers with a specific collection of $9g - 9$ curves $\big(\cP, \cQ, \cQ_+\big)$; see \cite[Theorem~4.10]{FLP} or \cite[p.~438]{FM}. \\

The collection of $9g - 9$ curves used in the proof of \cite[Thm.~10.7]{FM} has the following description. Let $\cP = \{a_1, \ldots, a_{3g - 3}\}$ be a pants decomposition. For each $a_j$, we consider a curve $a'_j$ (uniquely specified by the choice of $\Gamma$) that intersects $a_j$ but no other $a_i$, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{dual curve}. We let $a''_j$ to be the curve obtained from $+1$ Dehn twist\footnote{One may equally consider $-1$ Dehn twist or any other Dehn twist of $a_j'$ along $a_j$.} of $a'_j$ along $a_j$. The triple
$$
\big(\cP, \cQ, \cQ_+\big) = \Big( (a_j)_{j=1}^{3g - 3}, (a'_j)_{j=1}^{3g - 3}, (a''_j)_{j=1}^{3g - 3} \Big)
$$
is also used throughout this paper.
