\vskip.1in
The intersection numbers directly used in the proof of \cite[Theorem~4.10]{FLP}, however, are not all intersection numbers with closed curves in $\cS$. Given $(\cP,\Gamma)$, $3g-3$ of them are intersection numbers with the curves in $\cP$ and the others are intersection numbers with some local arcs in a thickening of some $c_j$. The same choice is used in \cite[P438]{FM} for coordinatizing measured foliations. More precisely, the set of curves $(K_j,K'_j,K''_j)_{j=1}^{3g-3}$ considered in \cite[P63, (I)(II)]{FLP} is the same as the triple $(\cP,\cQ,\cQ_+)=(a_j,a'_j,a''_j)_{j=1}^{3g-3}$ used in this paper. However, for some explicit closed cone $B$, the classifying maps
$$
\ov\Phi\colon \cF\lra B\subset \R^{9g-9}_{\geq 0} \quad \tn{and}\quad {\Phi}\colon \cS' \lra B \cap \N^{9g-9}
$$ 
defined  in \cite{FLP} do not rely on measures of/intersection numbers with the curves $a_j'$ and $a''_j$ from $\cQ$ and $\cQ_+$. Instead, ${\Phi}$ is defined using intersection numbers with the curves  $a_j$  in $\cP$, along with intersection numbers with local arcs $S_j$ and $T_j$ in thickenings of the curves $a_j$; see the definitions preceding \cite[Lemma~4.7]{FLP}. The function $\ov\Phi$ is defined similarly. Intersection numbers with  $S_j$ and $T_j$  satisfy simple equations in terms of twist coordinates along  $a_j$, making the map $\Phi$ easily expressible in terms of Dehn-Thurston coordinates. \\

Then, \cite[Appendix C]{FLP} shows that the twisting parameters $t_i$ can be found from $(n'_i,n''_i)$, but they do not provide explicit equations; see Proof Proposition~6.12. For the map $\ov\Phi$, in \cite[Appendix C]{FLP}, they show that measures along $(S_j,T_j)$ can be obtained from measures along $a'_j,a''_j$. This (c.f. \cite[Theorem~4.10]{FLP})  proves the existence of a closed cone  $\cC\subset \R_{\geq 0}^\cS$ and a continuous
map $\theta\colon \cC\to B$ that is positively homogeneous of degree one and that makes the following diagram commute:
$$
\xymatrix{
\cF \ar[rd]_{\ov\Phi}\ar[rr]^\iota &&\cC \ar[ld]^\theta \\
&B  &}
$$
Furthermore, $\theta$ induces a homeomorphism of $\iota(\cF)\subset \cC$ and $B$. However, as they point out in \cite[Sec~6.6]{FLP},  the restriction 
$$
\ov\Phi|_{\cS'}\colon \cS'\lra B\subset \N^{9g-9}
$$ 
does not coincide with $\Phi$. That is because the definition of $\Phi$ involves intersection numbers with arcs rather than closed curves, leading to some discontinuity issues. Therefore, one can not use the (inexplicit) formulas of \cite[Appendix C]{FLP} for $\theta^{-1}$ to write intersection numbers with $(S_j,T_j)$ in terms of intersection numbers with $(a'_j,a''_j)$. The reason for all this complication is stated as follows in \cite[P68]{FLP}: ``{\it It seems very diﬃcult to make these last formulas explicit.}"\\

{\it Addressing the shortcoming mentioned above, the following result provides explicit formulas for the intersection numbers with $a_j'$ and $a_j''$ in terms of DT coordinates. We use them to better understand the embedding of $\cS'$ into $\N^{9g-9}$ coming from intersection numbers with $(\cP,\cQ,\cQ_+)$, and to construct interesting filtrations on coordinate ring of the $\tn{SL}(2,\C)$-character variety associated to $\Si$.}

 \begin{theorem}\label{ThmA}
 Given a pants decomposition and an embedded dual graph $\Gamma$, for every $a\in \cP$ let $a'\in \cQ$ and $a''\in \cQ_+$ be as above.\newline
(I)  Suppose $a \in \cP$ belongs to two pairs of pants, and  $a_1,a_2,a_3,a_4$ are the other four curves in $\cP$ that belong to the two pairs of pants containing $a$ as in Figure~\ref{dual curve2}-Left. For every other multicurve $\mf{m}\in \cS'$, let 
$$
(n,t)=(n(\mf{m}),t(\mf{m}))\in \N\times \Z
$$ 
denote the intersection number of $\mf{m}$ with $a$ and the twist parameter of $\mf{m}$ along $a$ (i.e. Dehn-Thurston coordinates of $\mf{m}$ along $a$). Similarly, let $n_i$ (with $i=1,\ldots,4$) , $n'$, and $n''$ denote the intersection numbers of $\mf{m}$ with $a_i$, $a'$, and $a''$, respectively. Then,
\bEq{FI}
n'=f(n,t,n_1,n_2,n_3,n_4) \quad \tn{and}\quad n''=f(n,t-n,n_1,n_2,n_3,n_4) ,
\eEq
where 
$$
\aligned
f(n,t,n_1,n_2,n_3,n_4)&=\max \Big\{ \abs{2t+n-\wt{n}_1-\wt{n}_3}, \wt{n}_1+\wt{n}_3-n, \wt{n}_2+\wt{n}_4-n\Big\}\\
&\,+\de_{14}+\de_{23}+2(u_1+u_2+u_3+u_4)
\endaligned
$$
and 
$$
\aligned
&\de_{14}=\max\big\{ \frac{n_1+n_4 -n}{2},0\big\},\quad\de_{23}=\max\big\{ \frac{n_2+n_3 -n}{2},0\big\},\\
&u_1=\max\big\{ \frac{n_1-n_4 -n}{2},0\big\},\quad u_4=\max\big\{ \frac{n_4-n_1 -n}{2},0\big\}\\
&u_2=\max\big\{ \frac{n_2-n_3 -n}{2},0\big\},\quad u_3=\max\big\{ \frac{n_3-n_2 -n}{2},0\big\}\\
&\wt{n}_1= n_1-2u_1-\de_{14},\quad \wt{n}_4= n_4-2u_4-\de_{14},\\
&\wt{n}_2= n_2-2u_2-\de_{23}\quad \wt{n}_3= n_3-2u_3-\de_{23},\\
\endaligned
$$
\vskip.1in
(II)  Suppose $a\in\cP$ belongs to just one pair of pants, and  $a_1$ is the other curve in $\cP$ that belongs to the pairs of pants containing $a$ as in Figure~\ref{dual curve2}-Right. For every other multicurve $\mf{m}$, let $(n,t)$, $n_1$, $n'$, and $n''$ be as above. Then,
\bEq{FI}
n'=h(n,t,n_1) \quad \tn{and}\quad n''=h(n,t-n,n_1) ,
\eEq
where 
$$
h(n,t,n_1)=|t|+\max\Big\{\frac{n_1}{2}-n,0\Big\}.
$$

