 For every $k\geq 0$, let 
 $$
 \De_k\cong \{(x_0,\ldots,x_k) \in \R_{\geq 0}^{k+1}\colon \sum_{i=0}^k x_i =1\}
 $$
 denote the $k$-dimensional simplex. Given an affine space $A$,  (similarly to a simplicial complex) a {\bf polyherdal complex} in $A$ is a union of polygons glued along their boundary strata.\\

 From the results reviewed earlier in this section and Theorem~\ref{ThmA}, it is fairly straightforward to deduce the following statements.


\begin{theorem}\label{ThmB}
 Given a collection of $9g-9$ curves $\mc{C}=(\cP,\cQ,\cQ_+)$ as above, taking intersection numbers with the curves in $\cC$ yields an embedding 
$$
 \iota_\cC \colon \mathcal{S}' \lra \N^\cC
$$
that continuously extends to an embedding 
$$
\iota_\cC \colon \mathcal{F} \lra \R_{\geq 0}^\cC.
$$
The image of the second map is a union $\Lambda=\bigcup_{\ell}\La_\ell $ of $(6g-6)$-dimensional convex rational polyhedral cones  over a polyhedral complex  
$$
P=\bigcup_{\ell} P_\ell\subset \P(\R_{\geq 0}^\cC)\cong \De_{9g-10}
$$ 
that is homeomorphic to $S^{6g-7}$. The image of $\cS'$ in each $\Lambda_\ell$ is finite-index sub-monoid of $\Lambda_\ell\cap \N^{9g-9}$.
\end{theorem}


\subsection{Geometric P=W conjecture for $\tn{SL}_2(\C)$}
For any reductive Lie group $G$ and finitely generated group $\Gamma$, the $G$-{\bf character variety}
$$
\chi(\Gamma,G)=\tn{Hom}(\Gamma, G)\sslash G
$$ 
is the moduli space of $G$-representations of $\Gamma$ up to conjugation with elements of $G$. The special case $\chi_{g}(G)$ where $\Gamma=\pi_1(\Si_{g})$ is the fundamental group of a genus $g$ surface is well-studied and connects with several topics.
For instance, let $\cM_{g}(G)$ denote the so-called {\bf Dolbeault moduli space}, wherein the points parametrize flat principal $G$-Higgs bundles on closed Riemann surface of genus~$g$. The Dolbeault moduli space is hyperK\"ahler and admits a canonical  holomorphic (projective) Lagrangian fibration onto an affine space of half-dimension. There exists a canonical real analytic identification $\Psi\colon \cM_{g}(G)\lra \cX_{g}(G)$, called the {\it non-abelian Hodge correspondence}.\vskip.05in 

The geometric P=W conjecture, as proposed by Katzarkov et. al. \cite[Conj.~1.1]{KNPS}, and further refined in \cite{MMS},  aims to provide a deeper understanding of the asymptotic behavior of the transcendental map $\Psi$ with respect to suitable compactifications on both sides.




\begin{conjecture}\label{PWGeometric}
$\cX_{g}(G)$ admits a dlt log CY compactification, and the dual intersection complex of the boundary divisor is a polyhedral complex homeomorphic to a sphere.
\end{conjecture}

This conjecture extends to punctured Riemann surfaces and involves the interplay between ``relative" character varieties and parabolic Higgs bundles. In a recent work \cite{FF23}, Frohman and Tehrani used coordinates coming from intersection numbers with the arcs of an ideal triangulation to construct compactifications of character varieties of punctured surfaces with interesting properties. More explicitly, let $\Si_{g,n}$ denote an $n$-punctured genus $g$ surface. For $G= \tn{SL}(2,\C)$, negative of the trace function along the curves around punctures defines a fibration 
\begin{equation}\label{Rel-family}
\pi\colon  \cX_{g,n}:= \cX(\pi_{1}(\Si_{g,n},\tn{SL}_2(\C)) \lra \C^n, 
\end{equation}
whose fibers $\cX_{g,n,\mf{t}}=\pi^{-1}(\mf{t})$ are called the {\bf relative $\tn{SL}(2,\C)$-character varieties}. It is well-known that each $\cX_{g,n,\mf{t}}$ is quasi projective, hyperK\"ahler, and log Calabi-Yau. For other choices of $G$, the relative character varieties are defined by fixing the conjugacy classes of the representations at the punctures. 

\begin{theorem}[\cite{FF23}]\label{FF23theorem}
For $(g,n)$ with $n>0$ and $2g+n\geq 3$, an ``ideal triangulation" of $\Si_{g,n}$ defines a normal compactification $\ov{\cX}_{g,n}$ of $\cX_{g,n}$ such that the boundary divisor $D_{g,n}=\partial \ov{\cX}_{g,n}$ is an irreducible ample toric variety. Additionally, it defines a normal relative (i.e., fiber-wise) compactification $\ov{\cX}^{\tn{rel}}_{g,n}$ of the family (\ref{Rel-family}) such that for each $\mft\in \C^n$, the boundary divisor $D_{g,n,\mft}$ is a toric and $\mf{t}$-independent subvariety of $D_{g,n}$. Furthermore, the moment polytope complex of $D_{g,n,\mft}$ is a sphere.
\end{theorem}

A key point of the proof is that taking intersection numbers with the arcs of an ideal triangulation defines an embedding 
$$
\iota\colon \cS'\to \N^{6g-6+3n}
$$
whose image belongs to a rational polyhedral cone $\Lambda$. Furthermore, this map provides an isomorphism between the graded coordinate ring of ${\cX}_{g,n}$ and the algebra $\C[\iota(\cS')]$ associated to the monoid $\iota(\cS')\subset \Lambda$. We derive a similar conclusion from Theorem~\ref{ThmB} for closed Riemann surfaces, but the proof is significantly more complicated.


\begin{remark}
The homological P=W conjecture \cite{DHM} asserts that the non-abelian Hodge correspondence map exchanges the perverse and weight filtrations. Under certain technical assumptions, the geometric P=W conjecture implies the cohomological P=W conjecture at the highest weight level. It is noteworthy to mention that Katzarkov et al.'s initial conjecture posits that the dual intersection complex of the boundary divisor is homotopy equivalent to a sphere.  This weaker version  has been recently proved in the punctured case; c.f. \cite{Su}.
\end{remark}


\subsection{Connection between the Geometric P=W conjecture and  Theorem~\ref{ThmB}}\label{sec:conenction}

For $g,n\geq 0$, the \textbf{skein algebra} $\tn{Sk}_{A}(\Si_{g,n})$ of $\Si_{g,n}$ is the $\Z[q^\pm]$-algebra generated by isotopy classes of framed links in $\Si_{g,n}\times \R$ modulo the relations 
$$
\aligned
    \left<L\cup\KPA\right> &= (-q^{2}-q^{-2})\langle L\rangle
        &&\text{disjoint union with trivial loop}  \\
    \left<\KPB\right> &= q\left<\KPC\right> + q^{-1}\left<\KPD\right>
        &&\text{skein relation,} 
        \endaligned
$$ 
with the product structure defined by stacking two links on the top of each other. By \cite[Thm.~IX.7.1]{P1}, isotopy classes of multicurves (together with the empty multicurve  $\cong$ 1) form a basis of $\tn{Sk}_{q}(\Si_{g,n})$ as $\Z[q^\pm]$-module. On the other hand, by a Theorem of Bullock-Przytycki-Sikora \cite{Bu,PS} and Charles-March\'{e} \cite{ChMa}, the specialization $\tn{Sk}_{g,n}\defeq \tn{Sk}_{q=-1}(\Si_{g,n})$ of the skein algebra at $q = -1$ is canonically isomorphic, via the (negative of) trace function along loops, to the ring of regular functions $\C[\cX_{g,n}]$ of $\cX_{g,n}$. 
 The classical limit $\tn{Sk}_{q=1}(\Si_{g,n})$ is isomorphic to $\tn{Sk}_{q=-1}(\Si_{g,n})$, just not canonically. The isomorphism depends on a choice of a spin structure; see \cite{Barrett}. For simplicity, we will work with the $q=1$ classical limit.\\

 In the classical limit, the commutative product structure, denoted by $\ast$, is by taking union of two curves (in transverse position), and using the skein relations as reduction rules  
\bEq{skein-relation}
    \left<L\cup\KPA\right> =-2\langle L\rangle,\qquad 
    \left<\KPE\right> = \left<\KPC\right> + \left<\KPD\right> 
    \eEq
    to expand the product as a linear combination of multicurves. 
 As before, if $n=0$, we will simplify the notation $\tn{Sk}_{g,0}$ to $\tn{Sk}_{g}$. \\


The sum of intersection numbers with a finite collection of curves $\cC\subset \cS$ defines filtration 
$$
 F_0\subset F_1 \subset \cdots \subset \C[\cX_g]=\bigcup_{d\geq 0} F_d
$$
such that $F_d$ is the vector space generated by the set of multicurves $\mf{m}$ satisfying 
\begin{equation}\label{grading}
|\mf{m}|\defeq \sum_{c\in \cC}  i(\mf{m},c) \leq  d.
\end{equation}
To every such filtration, we can associate two graded algebras: 
\bEq{GAlg_e}
\tn{Sk}_{g}[u]\defeq \bigoplus_{d\geq 0} F_d u^d \quad \tn{and}\quad \tn{Sk}_{g}^{\tn{gd}}\defeq \bigoplus_{d\geq 0} F_d/F_{d-1}.
\eEq
where $u$ is an indeterminate over $\tn{Sk}_{g}$. We will denote the induced multiplication in the associated graded algebra $\tn{Sk}_{g}^{\tn{gd}}$ by $\overline{\ast}$. \\

By \cite[Proposition~2.8]{Mon}, for every such filtration
$$
\overline{\cX_g} \defeq \tn{Proj}(\tn{Sk}_{g}[u])
$$ 
is a reduced scheme containing $\cX_g$ as a Zariski dense open subset. The complement $\partial  \ov\cX_g=\ov\cX_g -\cX_g$ is the zero set of the ideal $\ll u\rr$ generated by $u$ and is isomorphic (as a scheme) to $\tn{Proj}(\tn{Sk}_{g}^{\tn{gd}})$. 
\begin{definition}
We call a filtration \textbf{finitely-generated} if the graded algebra $\tn{Sk}_{g}[u]$ is finitely generated. We call it \textbf{projective} if it is finitely generated and $F_0=\C$.
\end{definition}
 If the filtration is finitely generated, then $\ov\cX_g $ is a closed sub-scheme of a {\it generalized weighted projective space}\footnote{Following \cite[Definition~2.7]{Mon}, a generalized weighted projective space is a weighted projective space possibly with negative weights.} and $\partial \ov\cX_g $ is the support of an effective ample divisor. If in addition the filtration is a projective, then $\ov\cX_g $ is a projective variety.  The filtration coming from taking intersection with the collection of curves in Theorem~\ref{ThmB} has these properties.    \\

For every two multicurves $\mf{m}$ and $\mf{m}'$, let 
\bEq{decomposition}
\mf{m}\ast\mf{m}'=\sum_{s} \la_s \mf{m}_s,
\eEq
where $s$ runs over all possible smoothings of $\mf{m}\cup \mf{m}'$ (in general position) and the coefficient  $\la_s$ collects the contributions of trivial components (trivial curve $=-2$) appearing in a smoothing. Passing to $\tn{Sk}_{g}^{\tn{gd}}$, we get 
$$
\mf{m}~\ov\ast~\mf{m}'=\sum_{s\colon |\mf{m}_s|=|\mf{m}|+|\mf{m}'|} \la_s \mf{m}_s,
$$
\begin{theorem}\label{ThmC} 
With notation as in Theorem~\ref{ThmB}, \eqref{GAlg_e}, and \eqref{decomposition}, and for every two multicurves $\mf{m}$ and $\mf{m}'$, the followings hold.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $\iota_\cC(\mf{m})$ and $\iota_\cC(\mf{m}')$  belong to the same cone $\Lambda_\ell$, for some $\ell$, then there exists a unique term on the righthand side of (\ref{decomposition}), say $\mf{m}_\#$, satisfying $ \iota_\cC(\mf{m}_\#)\in \Lambda_\ell$ and
$$
\mf{m}\,\overline{\ast}\,\mf{m}'= \mf{m}_\#.
$$
\item If $\iota_\cC(\mf{m})$ and $\iota_\cC(\mf{m}')$ do not belong to the same cone $\Lambda_\ell$, for any $\ell$, then 
$$
\mf{m}\,\overline{\ast}\,\mf{m}'=0\in \tn{Sk}_{g}^{\tn{gd}}.
$$ 
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}


The key idea behind the proof is as follows. As mentioned earlier, Dehn–Thurston coordinates do not behave well under the product map ${\ast}$ in $\mathrm{Sk}_{g}$. In \cite{BKL}, the authors introduced a modified version of DT coordinates that satisfy the following “leading order term property” (see \cite[Theorem~9.1]{FKL23}):

\begin{quote}
For any two multicurves $\mf{m}$ and $\mf{m}'$, there is a unique term $\mf{m}_\#$ in their product whose modified DT coordinates are equal to the sum of the modified DT coordinates of $\mf{m}$ and~$\mf{m}'$.
\end{quote}

Modified DT coordinates are piecewise linear functions of the standard DT coordinates. Our key observation is that, after minor adjustments, the modified DT coordinates and the intersection coordinates on each cone $\Lambda_\ell$ are related by linear equations. This yields the first statement. The second follows from a convexity argument.\\



As a corollary of the previous theorem and Theorem~\ref{ThmB} we get the following theorem that is stronger than the prediction of the geometric P=W conjecture for $\mathrm{SL}(2, \mathbb{C})$-character varieties.

\begin{theorem}\label{ThmD}
For $g>1$, a collection of $9g-9$ curves $\mc{C}=(\cP,\cQ,\cQ_+)$ as above defines a normal projective compactification $\ov{\cX}_{g}$ of $\cX_{g}$ such that the boundary divisor $\partial \ov{\cX}_{g,n}$ is a union of toric orbifolds intersecting along toric strata. The intersection complex of $\partial \ov{\cX}_{g,n}$ is dual of the moment polytope complex
$$
P=\bigcup_{\ell} P_\ell \cong S^{6g-5}.
$$ 
Therefore the intersection complex of $\partial \ov{\cX}_{g,n}$ is homeomorphic to $S^{6g-7}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
In general, the resulting compactification $\ov\cX_g$ may not be dlt, but it can be made so by performing (toric) blowups that preserve the toric structure of the boundary divisor and maintain the polytope complex as a sphere. The log Calabi–Yau (log CY) property, however, is only known for punctured surfaces (see \cite{Wang}) and remains an open question for closed surfaces. We attempted the following approach in \cite{FF23}, which requires a deep understanding of the singularities.\\

Let $K$ denote the canonical divisor of $\ov\cX_g$. If we knew that (1) $\ov\cX_g$ is Cohen–Macaulay and (2) $\partial \ov\cX_g$ is $\Q$-Cartier, then, since (3) $\partial \ov\cX_g$ is ample, the log CY property could be deduced as follows (see \cite[Proposition~5.73]{MK} and \cite[Section~4.1]{Kollar}):\\

By adjunction and using the toric and spherical properties in Theorem~\ref{ThmD}, the restriction $\cO(K + \partial \ov\cX_g)|_{\ov\cX_g}$ is trivial. Since $\partial \ov\cX_g$ is ample, the Grothendieck–Lefschetz theorem then imply that $K + \partial \ov\cX_g$ itself is trivial.\\

However, condition (2) is often not satisfied. To address this, one would either need to perform a blowup (which compromises ampleness) or work with the notion of “different,” as studied in \cite[Section~4.1]{Kollar}. Both approaches -- as well as verifying condition (1) -- require a detailed understanding of the singularities, which is beyond the scope of this work.
\end{remark}

