Subtracting these $t$-independent contributions from $n'$ and removing the corresponding $U$- and $\beta$-arcs from the picture (which reduces $n_i$ to $\wt{n}_i$), Theorem~\ref{ThmA}.(I) reduces to the following simplified version.

\begin{proposition}\label{ThmA-simple}
(I) With notation as above, and supposing the curve $a$ belongs to two pairs of
pants, if $n \geq n_1 + n_4$ and $n \geq n_2 + n_3$, then
$$
n' = f(n,t,n_1,n_2,n_3,n_4) \quad \tn{and} \quad n'' = f(n,t-n,n_1,n_2,n_3,n_4),
$$
where
\bEq{simpleF}
f(n,t,n_1,n_2,n_3,n_4) = \max \Big\{ \abs{2t + n - n_1 - n_3},\; n_1 + n_3 - n,\; n_2 + n_4 - n \Big\}.
\eEq

(II) Similarly, if $a$ only belongs to one pair of pants in $\cP$ as in Figure~\ref{dual curve2}-Right, and $n \geq n_1/2$, then
$$
n' = |t| \quad \tn{and} \quad n'' = |t - n|.
$$
\end{proposition}

