%%%%%%%%% ABSTRACT
\begin{abstract} 
\label{Abstract}
AI-generated content (AIGC) enables efficient visual creation but raises copyright and authenticity risks. As a common technique for integrity verification and source tracing, digital image watermarking is regarded as a potential solution to above issues. Among these, watermarking methods capable of preserving the generation quality are receiving increased attention. However, the proliferation and high performance of generative image editing applications have elevated the risks of malicious tampering, creating new demands. 1) The tamper robustness of current lossless visual quality watermarks remains constrained by the modification-sensitive diffusion inversion process, necessitating enhanced robustness. 2) The improved tampering quality and rapid iteration cycles render passive tampering detection methods inadequate, making proactive tampering localization capability a desired feature for watermarks. To address these requirements, this paper proposes a \textbf{T}amper-\textbf{A}ware \textbf{G}enerative image \textbf{W}ater\textbf{M}arking method named TAG-WM. The proposed method comprises four key modules: a dual-mark joint sampling (DMJS) algorithm for embedding copyright and localization watermarks into the latent space while preserving generative quality, the watermark latent reconstruction (WLR) utilizing reversed DMJS, a dense variation region detector (DVRD) leveraging diffusion inversion sensitivity to identify tampered areas via statistical deviation analysis, and the tamper-aware decoding (TAD) guided by localization results. The experimental results indicate that TAG-WM achieves SOTA tampering robustness and tampering localization capability with distortions while maintaining lossless generation quality and a considerable capacity of 256 bits.
% The rise of AI-generated content (AIGC) has raised significant concerns regarding copyright protection and the authenticity of digital media. Image generation models like Stable Diffusion have democratized the ability to create high-quality images at minimal cost, but this also introduces challenges in protecting intellectual property and preventing misguiding information. Invisible watermarking and tampering localization are commonly used to address these issues. However, existing methods not only often compromise image quality or lead to longer inference time during the generation process, both degrading the user experience, but also fail to withstand image tampering attacks (ITAs) in watermarking, which can tamper the image while maintaining image usability, further increasing public concerns. ~In this paper, we present a novel method that simultaneously embeds information watermarks and tampering localization templates into diffusion-generated images without degrading image quality and with negligible calculation time during the generation process. To realize tampering localization in this situation, we introduce the High-Frequency Changes Detector (HFCD). Based on our analysis and experiments, our accurate tampering localization results can also be used to enhance watermark robustness against ITAs. Our method operates in a fully blind manner, meaning it does not require auxiliary information for watermark extraction or tampering localization, offering an efficient and scalable solution for real-world copyright protection and traceability applications.

%\noindent Keywords: AI-generated content, Diffusion models, Watermarking, Copyright protection, Provenance Tracking, Image Tampering Localization

\end{abstract}
