\mzh{Meanwhile, the emergence of generative models like ControlNet \cite{DMs-ControlNet}, which enable high-quality image modifications, has made malicious tampering an increasingly critical issue, imposing stricter requirements for watermark robustness and tamper localization capabilities. Unlike common image distortions, malicious alterations fundamentally alter pixel values. Unfortunately, the DDIM inversion process demonstrates sensitivity to pixel modifications (Sec. \ref{sec:smp_ddim}), rendering inversion-based generative watermarking methods like GS less robust against image manipulation distortions (e.g., cropping, tampering). On the other hand, as generative model-based image manipulations become increasingly indistinguishable, applying watermarks to achieve more generalizable proactive tamper localization has emerged as a novel and practical demand in watermark functionality. For instance, MaLP \cite{tl-proactive-MaLP} achieves tamper detection and pixel-level localization through learned template embedding. EditGuard \cite{wm_and_tl-Editguard} simultaneously embeds both identification watermarks and tampering localization watermarks into images, decoding them in parallel to accomplish dual objectives of copyright verification and tamper localization.}
%To combat image tampering attacks, several advanced tampering localization algorithms have been developed. These methods are typically classified into two categories: active detection and passive detection. Most existing methods, such as MVSS-Net and PSCC-Net, belong to passive detection, where the model is trained to distinguish between tampered and original regions in an image. However, these methods often lack generalization capabilities and struggle to transfer effectively across datasets with different distributions. Active detection, on the other hand, involves embedding additional information into the image to facilitate tampering localization. EditGuard is one such method, which sequentially embeds both watermark and tampering localization templates into images or videos and decodes them in parallel to achieve both copyright detection and tampering localization. However, to ensure good generalization performance, these methods often require training on diverse datasets, and like many post-processing watermarking techniques, they can suffer from image quality degradation and additional computational costs.

\mzh{To address the aforementioned challenges while introducing tamper localization capabilities, we propose a tamper-aware generative image watermarking named TAG-WM. This framework leverages the sensitivity of DDIM inversion to pixel modifications to design tamper localization strategies, and further employs the localization results as confidence guidance for watermark decoding, thereby enhancing the accuracy of watermark extraction under tampering distortions.}

%This leads us to consider whether it is possible to simultaneously embed information watermarks and tampering localization templates without degrading image quality, while also improving watermark robustness against image tampering attacks. The answer is yes. Inspired by Gaussian Shading, we propose a pre-processing embedding approach that, while maintaining lossless image quality during generation, embeds both watermarks and localization templates simultaneously. Using a series of optimization algorithms, we achieve precise tampering localization and improved robustness of the watermark against ITAs. Moreover, our algorithm incurs minimal extra computation, making it highly efficient. Compared to post-processing watermarking and tampering localization models, our approach maintains user experience while achieving equivalent results. It also boasts superior generalization performance, ensuring that any image generated by our model can effectively support tampering localization and watermark protection, regardless of data distribution. Notably, our method operates in a fully blind manner, meaning that tampering localization and watermark extraction do not require any additional information, making the process seamless.

The main contributions of this paper are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item We propose a dual-mark joint sampling algorithm to simultaneously embed copyright watermark and localization watermark into the latent space of diffusion models. This strategy preserves standard normal distributions of latent representation, ensuring lossless visual quality while introducing tampering localization capability.

    \item We develop a dense variation region detector for tampering localization, leveraging the sensitivity of diffusion inversion to image modifications. By analyzing statistical deviations between original/reconstructed localization watermarks, the proposed detection method demonstrates strong generalization capability.

    \item We introduce tamper-aware message decoding guided by tampering localization results, which improves the robustness of such generated image watermarking methods against image modifications.
\end{itemize}

