\subsection{Denoising Diffusion Implicit Model}
The Denoising Diffusion Implicit Model (DDIM) accelerates diffusion-based generation by defining a non-Markovian process. It enables deterministic sampling through a modified reverse process. The generation process can be formulated as:
\begin{equation}
\scalebox{0.9}{$
    %v4
    x_{t-1} = \sqrt{\frac{\bar\alpha_{t-1}}{\bar\alpha_t}}x_t+(\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_{t-1}}-\sqrt{\frac{\bar\alpha_{t-1}(1-\bar\alpha_{t})}{\bar\alpha_{t}}})\epsilon_{\theta}^{(t)}(x_t)
    %v3
    % x_{t-\Delta t} = \sqrt{\frac{\alpha_{t-\Delta t}}{\alpha_t}}x_t+(\sqrt{1-\alpha_{t-\Delta t}}-\sqrt{\frac{\alpha_{t-\Delta t}(1-\alpha_{t})}{\alpha_{t}}})\epsilon_{\theta}^{(t)}(x_t)
    %v2
    % x_{t-\Delta t} = \sqrt{\frac{\alpha_{t-\Delta t}}{\alpha_t}}x_t+\frac{1}{2}(\frac{1-\alpha_{t-\Delta t}}{\alpha_{t-\Delta t}}-\frac{1-\alpha_{t}}{\alpha_{t}})\cdot\sqrt{\frac{\alpha_t\alpha_{t-\Delta t}}{1-\alpha_t}}\epsilon_{\theta}^{(t)}(x_t)
    % v1
    % x_{t-1} = \sqrt{\alpha_{t-1}} {\hat{x}}_0 + \sqrt{1 - \alpha_{t-1} - \sigma_t^2} \epsilon_\theta({x}_t, t) + \sigma_t \epsilon.
    $}
\end{equation}
Suppose $\epsilon_{\theta}^{(t)}(x_t)\approx\epsilon_{\theta}^{(t-1)}(x_{t-1})$, then the inversion process is as follows:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:DDIM_inversion}
\scalebox{0.87}{$
    % v4
    x_{t} \approx \sqrt{\frac{\bar\alpha_{t}}{\bar\alpha_{t-1}}}x_{t-1}+(\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_{t}}-\sqrt{\frac{\bar\alpha_{t}(1-\bar\alpha_{t-1})}{\bar\alpha_{t-1}}})\epsilon_{\theta}^{(t-1)}(x_{t-1})
    % v1
    % x_t = \sqrt{\alpha_t} {x}_0 + \sqrt{1 - \alpha_t} \epsilon_\theta({x}_t, t).
    $}
\end{equation}
which we used to recover watermarked pseudo-noise.

\subsection{Spatial Mapping Properties}
\label{sec:smp_ddim}
According to the mapping relationship Eq.~(\ref{eq:DDIM_inversion}) between the noise $x_t$ and the image $x_0$ in the DDIM framework, it is evident that modifications in the pixel space of $x_0$ will affect the value of the corresponding positions in $x_t$. This characteristic is preserved to some extent in the VAE-based latent-diffusion architecture \cite{DMs-LDMs} because the VAE is designed to accelerate inference, maintaining the pixel structure. As a result, spatial perturbations in the image domain result in changes at the corresponding positions in the latent space during inversion. %\mzh{Our experimental results show that xxxxx, substantiating this conclusion; see Appendix AAA.}

