\section{Conclusion}
\label{Conclusion}
In this paper, we present the first in-generation image watermarking framework that integrates copyright message embedding and tamper localization within diffusion models. Our approach achieves several notable advancements: first, we introduce parallel watermark embedding, which eliminates the mutual interference between the two watermarks and provides a 4$\times$ increase in capacity compared to post-processing methods like EditGuard, without any loss in image quality. Second, we propose a tampering-aware optimization strategy, which dynamically adjusts watermark robustness based on tampering localization, resulting in a 7-10\% improvement in tamper resistance across various tampering scenarios compared to state-of-the-art methods. Finally, our framework is highly efficient, operating at just 10.78ms per image during generation, which is 6.3× faster than the post-processing approach EditGuard.
However, the reliance on deterministic DDIM inversion limits compatibility with other SDE schedulers, and like existing in-generation methods, our framework remains vulnerable to full-image adversarial attacks that globally distort noise patterns. And using a fixed seed to initilize localization watermarks may lead it vulnerable to malicious attacks. Future work involves enhancing fine-grained tampering detection through attention-guided watermark allocation and developing adaptive dynamic watermark strategies.

%Looking forward, there are promising avenues for future work. Incorporating attention-guided watermark allocation could enhance the precision of fine-grained tampering detection. Furthermore, the development of dynamic watermark strategies that adapt to different security levels could offer more flexibility in protecting generated content, paving the way for more secure and controllable AIGC systems.


