\section{The operator $P^+$}
\label{app:P+}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
It is possible to define an alternate permutation-like operator $P^+$ in place of $P^-$ in \eqref{eq:P-Majorana} as
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:P-Majorana-+}
    P^+_{jk} = \frac{\gamma_j+\gamma_k}{\sqrt{2}}.
\end{eqnarray}
Here too, the inverse is itself
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:P-inverse-Majorana-+}
   (P^+)^{-1}_{jk}=P^+_{jk} = \frac{\gamma_j+\gamma_k}{\sqrt{2}}. 
\end{eqnarray}
However in this case it satisfies the braided version of a modified YBE
$$ P^+_{ij}P^+_{jk}P^+_{ij}=-P^+_{jk}P^+_{ij}P^+_{jk} = P^-_{ik},  $$
known as the {\it anti-YBE} \cite{PADMANABHAN2024116664}. It does not satisfy the non-braided YBE or non-braided anti-YBE. Due to this fact we cannot use this operator for converting a spectral parameter dependent braided YBO into a non-braided YBO. Thus its use for obtaining integrable models this way is limited.

Just as the $P^-$ operator it satisfies a far-anticommutativity relation
\begin{eqnarray}
    P^+_{ij}P^+_{kl} = -P^+_{kl}P^+_{ij}, 
\end{eqnarray}
instead of the far-commutativity relation. Both these relations distinguishes it from the usual permutation operator. The consequence is also seen in the way this permutation interchanges other Majorana fermion operators. The action is by conjugation
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:ptilde+-action-majoranas}
    &P^+_{jk}\gamma_jP^+_{jk} = \gamma_k~;~ P^+_{jk}\gamma_k P^+_{jk} = \gamma_j, & \nonumber \\
    &  P^+_{jk}\gamma_l P^+_{jk} = -\gamma_l,~~~\textrm{when}~l\neq j,k.   &
\end{eqnarray}

