\section{Baseline Stabilizer for a Satellite Swarm in a Coplanar Equidistant Plane}
\label{Problem_Formulation}
This section develops a baseline orbital stabilizer for a satellite swarm with equal spacing in a single orbital plane. We first define the averaged $J_2$ relative orbital parameters to capture the averaged relative orbital motion under $J_2$ gravity. Then, this section derives its target orbital parameters along with the target plane as ``Swarm Plane $\Phi(\Theta_{P},\Theta_{z-xy})$'' and designs an orbital stabilizer to achieve a distributed space structure in coplanar equidistant. %Their coordinates and specific disturbances. ignoring the distance constraint,
%\subsection{Target Orbital-Plane Description}
%\label{Target_Trajectory_Description}
\subsection{Averaged $J_2$ Relative Orbital Parameters}
\label{J2_Relative_Orbital_Parameters}
First, we introduce passive stable trajectories in Earth's orbit and derive the averaged $J_2$ relative orbital parameters that are crucial for the following controller design. From the differential equation of the averaged relative orbit dynamics in Eq.~(\ref{Hill_dynamics}), we calculated the analytical solution of the future trajectory predicted at time $t=0$ under no input:
\begin{equation}
\label{CWsol}
\begin{aligned}
%\overline{p}(t)=
&\begin{bmatrix}
    {x}(t)\\
    {y}(t)\\
    {z}(t)
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
r_{\mathrm{o}}(0,t)\\
0
\end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
   r_{xy}\sin{(\omega_{xy} t + \theta_{xy})}/c_+\\
    2r_{xy}\cos{(\omega_{xy} t + \theta_{xy})}/c_-\\
    (r_z+l_z t) \sin{({\omega}_z t+\theta_z)}
\end{bmatrix}\\
&r_{\mathrm{o}}(0,t)=
\begin{bmatrix}
    {2C_{1}}&{C_4-\epsilon_{2} C_1 t}
\end{bmatrix}^\top,\ \epsilon_2 = \frac{4c_+^2-c_-^2}{c_+c_-}\omega_{xy}
%= \frac{4c_+^2-c_-^2}{c_+c_-}\omega_{xy}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation} 
where $3+5s_{J_2}=4c_+^2-c_-^2$, and its time differentiation yields Eq.~(\ref{Hill_dynamics}); $c_{\pm}$ is given by Eq. ~(\ref{Hill_dynamics}); and $r_{\mathrm{o}}(0,t)\in\mathbb{R}^2$ is the center position of the relative orbit at time $t$ predicted at $t=0$. The orbital indices computed at estimation time ($t=0$) are defined as follows:
\begin{equation}
\label{definition_C}
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
C_{1}&={c_+}/{c_-^2}(2 \overline{x}+{\dot{\overline{y}}}/{ {\omega}_{xy}}),\ C_{3}=\overline{x}-2c_+C_{1}\\
C_{4}&= (\overline{y}-{2 \dot{ \overline{x}}}/{{\omega}_{xy}})/c_-,\ C_{2}=( \overline{y}-c_-C_4)/2,\\
r_{xy}^2&= {C_2^2+C_3^2},\ \theta_{xy}= \tan^{-1}(C_3,C_2),\\ 
C_5&= \dot{z}/\omega_z,\ C_6 = z,\\
r_{z}^2&= {C_6^2+C_5^2},\ \theta_{z}= \tan^{-1}(C_6,C_5).\\
%&,\ C_{5}=\frac{\dot{z}_0}{\omega},\ C_{6}=z_0
\end{aligned}
\right.
\end{equation}
As for operational convenience, the remaining ``Time'' to escape from a controllable range is sometimes a more valuable index for safety mode than distance. We provide the ``relaxed'' connectable time $\hat{T}_{\mathrm{conn}}(C_{1,4})$ for a deputy satellite to exit from a controllable region based on the analytical solution in Eq.~(\ref{CWsol}). Please refer to subsection \ref{Escape_Connectable_Time_Relaxation} in the Appendix for more accurate definitions that require computational loads. 
\begin{definition}[Connectable Time]  
%For connection conditions at initial time, i.e., $r_s^2-(2C_1)^2\geq C_4^2$, 
we define $\hat{T}_{\mathrm{conn}}(C_{1,4})$, where a given $r_s$ coincides with the distance of the ellipse centers $\|r_{\mathrm{o}}(t)\|$, i.e., $\hat{T}_{\mathrm{conn}}(C_{1,4})=\left\{t \geq 0\ |\ \|r_{\mathrm{o}}(t)\|^2= r_s^2\right\}$. The positive and negative solutions are as follows: 
\begin{equation}
\label{T_conn}
\hat{T}_{\mathrm{conn}}(C_{1,4})
%&=\left\{t \geq t_0\ |\ (2C_1)^2+(C_4-3\omega C_1 t)^2= r_s^2\right\}\\
%&\Rightarrow  \hat{T}_{\mathrm{conn}}(C_{1,4})% = \frac{C_4 + \mathrm{sign}(C_1)\sqrt{r_s^2 - (2C_1)^2}}{3\omega C_1}
=\max\left(\max\left(\frac{C_4\pm \sqrt{r_s^2-(2C_1)^2}}{\epsilon_2 C_1}\right),0\right).
%=\frac{\mathrm{sgn}(C_1)C_4 + \sqrt{r_s^2 - (2C_1)^2}}{3\omega |C_1|}\geq 0
\end{equation}
Note that although $\hat{T}_{\mathrm{conn}}\rightarrow\infty$ as $\|r_{\mathrm{o}}(0,t)\| \rightarrow 0$, the converse is not guaranteed since $C_1=0$ yields $\hat{T}_{\mathrm{conn}}\rightarrow \infty$.% but $\|r_{\mathrm{o}}(0,t)\| \neq 0$.
\end{definition}
