where $\psi,k_1,f_{0},g_{0}$ are presented in Eq.~(\ref{coefficient_condition}). We note that ${\overline{r}}_{xy(0,0,1,0)}^2=r_{\mathrm{xy}}^2$, $\overline{r}_{xy(2c_+,c_-/2,1,0)}^2=\overline{x}^2+(\overline{y}/2)^2$, and ${\overline{r}}_{xy({c_-\epsilon_2}/{(2\omega_{xy})},0,1,0)}^2=({\dot{\overline{x}}}/{\omega_{xy}})^2+({\dot{\overline{y}}}/{(2\omega_{xy})})^2$. It is worth noting that the above satisfies $U_j =E[\cdot]$, which indicates that $[C_i] = [C_i] -\frac{\mathbf{1}^\top C_i}{n}\mathbf{1}$. By selecting $(\varrho,\varsigma,\tau,\psi)=(2c_+,0,1,0)$ and $\hat{E}=E$ and applying this input, the following time derivative of $V_{\mathrm{all}}$ is derived:
$$
\begin{aligned}
