\begin{document}
\title{Set-Based Control Barrier Functions and Safety Filters}%: A Unifying Framework for Safety Filters}
\author{Kim P. Wabersich, Felix Berkel, Felix Gruber, Sven Reimann
    \thanks{The authors are with Corporate Research Robert Bosch GmbH, 71272 Renningen, Germany. (E-mail: {firstname.lastname}@bosch.com).}
    %\thanks{*As part of the ``AUTOtech.agil'' project (FKZ 01IS22088Y), this work is supported by the Federal Ministry of Education and Research of Germany (BMBF).}
}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
    High performance and formal safety guarantees are common requirements for industrial control applications. Control barrier function (CBF) methods provide a systematic approach to the modularization of safety and performance. However, the design of such CBFs can be challenging, which limits their applicability to large-scale or data-driven systems. This paper introduces the concept of a set-based CBF for linear systems with convex constraints. By leveraging control invariant sets from reachability analysis and predictive control, the set-based CBF is defined implicitly through the minimal scaling of such a set to contain the current system state. This approach enables the development of implicit, data-driven, and high-dimensional CBF representations. The paper demonstrates the design of a safety filter using set-based CBFs, which is suitable for real-time implementations and learning-based approximations to reduce online computational demands. The effectiveness of the method is illustrated through comprehensive simulations on a high-dimensional mass-spring-damper system and a motion control task, and it is validated experimentally using an electric drive application with short sampling times, highlighting its practical benefits for safety-critical control.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
    Control barrier functions, Safety-critical control, Invariant sets, NL Predictive control
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Modern industrial control systems are increasingly required to deliver high performance, such as energy efficiency, comfort, and precise reference tracking while simultaneously guaranteeing safety under stringent operational constraints. Achieving this balance is particularly challenging, as safety requirements often necessitate conservative designs that limit achievable performance. Safety filters have emerged as a modular and permissive supervisory layer that can be integrated with high-performance controllers, human operators, or learning-based policies, as illustrated, e.g., in~\cite{wabersich2023}. These filters act as real-time certifiers, ensuring that safety specifications are maintained even in the presence of potentially unsafe control actions.
%
Various research approaches have been developed for the design of safety filters, all of which build on the concept of set invariance, see, e.g., \cite{wabersich2023} for an overview. Reachability-based methods~\cite{fisac2018general} involve set-based propagation of all potential system trajectories, control barrier functions (CBF)~\cite{ames2016control} are based on concepts from Lyapunov theory to identify inputs for set invariance, and predictive control techniques~\cite{wabersich2021probabilistic} use a receding horizon optimal control problem to ensure recursive constraint satisfaction at each control sampling time step.
%
\par
%
Recent research has highlighted the potential of combining these approaches to leverage their complementary strengths~\cite{wabersich2023}. However, a unified framework that systematically integrates reachability analysis, CBFs, and predictive control remains an open problem, primarily due to the differing assumptions and mathematical structures inherent to each method.
%
\paragraph*{Contributions}
This work introduces a unifying set-based CBF framework for safety filter design in linear systems with convex state and input constraints. The proposed approach constructs CBFs via the minimal scaling of a control invariant set that contains the current state, thereby enabling the use of scalable, implicitly defined, or data-driven safe sets such as zonotopes, polytopes, convex hulls of trajectories, or feasible sets of predictive controllers. This generalization overcomes the limitations of classical CBFs, which typically require explicit (analytic) representations of the safe set.
%
The resulting set-based CBF safety filter inherits the favorable properties of conventional CBF-based designs, including the ability to shape the closed-loop behavior near the safe set boundary through the selection of class $\mathcal{K}$ functions~\cite{agrawal2017discrete,wabersich22,alan2023control} (\cref{thm:nominal_set_cbf}). Moreover, continuity of the set-based CBF ensures asymptotic stability of the safe set under the safety filter, provided the underlying set is robust control invariant with respect to small perturbations~(\cref{thm:set_based_cbf}).
%
\par
%
We present a systematic procedure for incorporating set-based CBF constraints into safety filter optimization problems (\cref{sec:design_and_implementation}), covering a range of invariant set representations, including polytopes in both vertex and half-space forms, zonotopes, and predictive safe sets. The resulting safety filter problems are amenable to efficient real-time implementation using standard optimization tools. Furthermore, we demonstrate that the set-based CBF can be efficiently approximated using learning-based methods, thereby reducing online computational requirements without compromising safety guarantees (\cref{subsec:approximation_set_based_cbf}). By leveraging techniques from stochastic MPC, we also provide a straightforward extension toward stochastic disturbances (\cref{sec:stochastic}).
%
The effectiveness and scalability of the proposed framework are validated through comprehensive simulations (\cref{sec:numerical_simulation}), including a high-dimensional mass-spring-damper system and a motion control task, as well as experimental results on an electric drive system with short sampling times (\cref{sec:electrical_machine}).
%
\paragraph*{Related Work}
%
Scalable safe set computations for safety filter design have been addressed through reachability-based methods~\cite{gruber2023scalable}, system-level synthesis~\cite{leeman2023predictive}, and predictive control techniques~\cite{wabersich2021probabilistic,gurriet2018towards}. While these methods enable the efficient construction of safe sets for complex and high-dimensional systems, they do not inherently provide CBF-based mechanisms for shaping the closed-loop behavior near the boundary of the safe set. In contrast, the set-based CBF framework introduced in this work combines the advantages of scalable safe set computation with the ability to systematically influence closed-loop dynamics, and can be efficiently designed to guarantee both safety and asymptotic stability of the safe set.
%
\par
%
Recent advances in integrating CBFs with predictive control have been reported in~\cite{wabersich2023}, where the sum of slack variables from a soft-constrained predictive control problem is utilized as a CBF. Although this approach guarantees asymptotic stability of the safe set, it does not provide a systematic means to tune the closed-loop behavior near the safe set boundary, as is standard in the CBF literature~\cite{alan2023control}. In contrast, the set-based CBF framework introduced in this paper generalizes predictive control methods by uniformly scaling the constraints of the underlying problem for any given state, yielding a CBF that is continuously differentiable in the vicinity of the safe set boundary. This smoothness not only enables more precise shaping of the closed-loop response but also facilitates efficient and accurate learning-based approximations, compared to, e.g.,~\cite{didier2023approximate}. While a related mechanism has been explored in~\cite{didier2024approximate} for predictive safety filters, that approach relies on iterative constraint tightening and the availability of a classical CBF for design. In contrast, our method enables the direct transformation of any nominal or robust recursively feasible MPC formulation into a set-based CBF, broadening its applicability and simplifying the design process for linear systems with convex constraints.
%
\par
%
Connections between reachability-based safety frameworks and CBFs have been explored in~\cite{choi2021robust} to enhance closed-loop performance with less conservative safety interventions. While this approach is capable of handling uncertain nonlinear system dynamics, it relies on Hamilton-Jacobi reachability analysis, which requires solving partial differential equations and is therefore computationally tractable only for systems with low-dimensional state spaces.
%
\par
%
In summary, while previous research has explored various combinations of safety filter design methods and control barrier functions - often in a fragmented manner (see also~\cite{wabersich2023} for a comprehensive overview) - a unified framework has remained elusive. By focusing on linear systems, this paper combines the strengths of these approaches into a cohesive methodology, providing efficient design procedures for set-based CBFs that guarantee asymptotic stability of the safe set.
%
\paragraph*{Outline of this article}
%
\cref{sec:problem_setting} formalizes the system class, constraints, and safety filter problem considered in this article, and \cref{sec:cbf} recalls the concept of CBFs and their discrete-time counterparts, which are used to design a safety filter based on CBFs. We then introduce set-based CBFs in \cref{sec:set_based_cbf}. \cref{sec:design_and_implementation} provides a general procedure for incorporating set-based CBF constraints into safety filter problems, and shows how to design set-based CBFs using common control invariant set computations. \cref{subsec:approximation_set_based_cbf} introduces an efficient learning procedure to approximate set-based CBFs with robustness guarantees to reduce online computational requirements. Finally, we demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed framework through simulations (\cref{sec:numerical_simulation}) and experiments (\cref{sec:electrical_machine}).
%
\paragraph*{Notation}
Let $c\mathcal A\triangleq \{ca | a\in\mathcal A\}$ for some set $\mathcal A\subseteq\R^n$ and scalar $c\in\R$. A continuous function $\alpha: [0, a) \rightarrow \R$  for some $a>0$ is said to be class $\mathcal K$ $(\alpha \in \mathcal K)$ if it is strictly increasing and $\alpha(0) = 0$ and is said to be extended class $\mathcal K$ ($\alpha\in\mathcal K^e$) if it is a class $\mathcal K$ function defined on $(-a,b)$ with $a,b>0$. A ball of radius $r>0$ defined by the norm $\Vert . \Vert$ is denoted by $\mathcal B_n(r)\triangleq \{ x\in\mathbb R^n~|~\Vert x \Vert \leq r\}$. The Minkowski sum of two sets $\mathcal A, \mathcal B\subseteq\R^n$ is defined as $\mathcal A\oplus \mathcal B\triangleq \{a+b~|~a\in\mathcal A, b\in\mathcal B\}$.
The Pontryagin difference of two sets $\mathcal A, \mathcal B\subseteq\R^n$ is defined as $\mathcal A\ominus \mathcal B\triangleq \{x\in\R^n~|~x\oplus\mathcal B\subseteq\mathcal A\}$. The interior of a set $\mathcal A \subseteq \R^n$ is denoted by $\mathrm{int}(\mathcal A)$.
%
\section{Preliminaries}\label{sec:preliminaries}
%
\subsection{Problem Setting}\label{sec:problem_setting}
%% [System dynamics]
We consider linear, time-invariant systems of the form
\begin{align}\label{eq:linear_system}
    x(k+1) = Ax(k) + Bu(k),
\end{align}
with states $x(k)\in\mathbb R^{n_x}$ and inputs $u(k)\in \mathbb R^{n_u}$ at time step $k$.
% [Constraints]
System~\cref{eq:linear_system} is subject to state and input constraints
    \begin{align}\label{eq:constraints}
        x(k) \in \mathcal X, \qquad 
        u(k) \in \mathcal U,
    \end{align}
with compact and convex sets $\mathcal X \subset \mathbb R^{n_x}$ and $\mathcal U \subset \mathbb R^{n_u}$ containing the origin. An extension to the case of additive stochastic disturbances is outlined in \cref{subsec:approximation_set_based_cbf} and omitted for brevity in the following.
%
\par
%% [Motivation for the use of Safety Filters]
Safety filters $\kappa_{\mathrm f}:\R^{n_x}\times\R^{n_u}\rightarrow \R^{n_u}$ are concerned with ensuring that the safety specifications~\cref{eq:constraints} are satisfied when the system is presented with potentially unsafe desired control inputs $u_{\mathrm{des}}(k)$.
%% [Concept of safety filters].
Instead of applying $u_{\mathrm{des}}(k)$ directly to the system, the safety filter $\kappa_{\mathrm f}(x(k), u_{\mathrm{des}}(k))$ monitors the current system state $x(k)$ and the desired control input signals $u_{\mathrm{des}}(k)$ and only applies them if safety can be certified. Otherwise, the safety filter overrides $u_{\mathrm{des}}(k)$ to produce a safe control signal with minimal intervention so that the constraints will be satisfied according to~\cref{eq:constraints} for all future times.
%
\par
%
Two key properties are desirable for practical safety filters~\cite{wabersich2023} and will be the goal of this article. First, the filter should be permissive, that is, it should interfere minimally with the desired control input while providing safety. Second, it should provide a way to tune how `fast' a system can approach its safety limits.
%
In the following sections, we introduce CBF-based safety filters and define different kinds of set invariance used throughout this article. Then, we show how to leverage safe sets from scalable reachability analysis and predictive control to construct the underlying CBFs efficiently.
%
\subsection{Safety Filters based on Control Barrier Functions}\label{sec:cbf}
%
Safety filters using CBFs have, e.g., been proposed in~\cite{gurriet2018towards,choi2021robust,didier2023approximate} and are given by the optimal input resulting from the following optimization problem:
\begin{subequations}
    \label{eq:cbf_filter}
    \begin{align}
        \min_{u\in\mathcal U} ~    & G(u, u_{\mathrm{des}}) \label{eq:cbf_filter_objective}\\
        \mathrm{s.t.} ~ & h(Ax + Bu) - h(x) \geq -\Delta h(x),\label{eq:cbf_filter_decrease_condition}
    \end{align}
\end{subequations}
that is,
\begin{align}\label{eq:cbf_safety_filter_law}
    \kappa_{\mathrm{f}}(x,u_{\mathrm{des}})\triangleq u^*(x,u_{\mathrm{des}}),
\end{align}
with $u^*(x,u_{\mathrm{des}})$ denoting the optimal solution to~\cref{eq:cbf_filter}, given a state $x$.
%
A commonly applied filtering objective is given by $G(u, u_{\mathrm{des}}) = \Vert u - u_{\mathrm{des}} \Vert$. Other objective functions in literature account for different norms and include future desired inputs, see, e.g.,~\cite{wabersich2023}. 
%
Safety with respect to the system constraints~\cref{eq:constraints} is encoded in the so-called CBF constraint~\cref{eq:cbf_filter_decrease_condition}, based on a discrete-time CBF $h$~\cite[Definition III.1]{wabersich22} defined as follows.
%
\begin{definition}[Control Barrier Function]\label{def:barrier_function}
    A function $h:\mathcal D\rightarrow\R$ with compact domain~$\mathcal D\subset\R^{n_x}$ is a \gls{CBF} with a corresponding safe set $\mathcal S\triangleq \{x\in\R^{n_x}:h(x)\geq 0\}\subseteq \mathcal D$ for \cref{eq:linear_system} and \cref{eq:constraints}, if $\mathcal S\subseteq\mathcal X$ and $\mathcal D$ are non-empty and compact, $h(x)$ is continuous on $\mathcal D$, and if there exists a function $\alpha\in\mathcal K^e$ such that for all $x\in\mathcal D$ there exists an input $u\in\mathcal U$ satisfying
    \begin{align}\label{eq:cbf_condition}
        h(Ax + Bu)-h(x) \geq -\Delta h(x),
    \end{align}
    with
    \begin{align}\label{eq:cbf_decrease_bound}
        \Delta h(x) \triangleq
        \begin{cases}
            \min(\alpha(h(x)),h(x)), & \text{if } h(x)\geq 0,\\
            \max(\alpha(h(x)),h(x)), & \text{else.}
        \end{cases}
    \end{align}
    The set of safe control inputs at $x\in\mathcal S$ w.r.t. $h$ is given by
    \begin{align}\label{eq:cbf_safe_control}
        K_{\mathrm{CBF}}(x)\triangleq \{u\in\mathcal U~|~\cref{eq:cbf_condition}\}.
    \end{align}
\end{definition}
%
\par
%
Based on \cref{def:barrier_function}, the CBF constraint~\cref{eq:cbf_filter_decrease_condition} ensures that the value of the CBF along closed-loop trajectories does not decrease `too' rapidly, when starting within the safe set, i.e., $x\in\mathcal S$. Specifically, the first case in~\cref{eq:cbf_decrease_bound} permits a maximal decrease of $h(x)$ for $h(x)\geq 0$, thereby guaranteeing that $h(Ax+B\kappa_{\mathrm{cbf}}(x,u_{\mathrm{des}}))\geq 0$, which implies $Ax+B\kappa_{\mathrm{cbf}}(x,u_{\mathrm{des}}) \in \mathcal S$.
%
If the system state is outside the safe set, i.e., $x\notin\mathcal S$ due to disturbances or unsafe initial conditions, the second case in~\cref{eq:cbf_decrease_bound} enforces a monotonic increase of the CBF value $h(x)$ along trajectories, thereby ensuring asymptotic stability of the safe set.
%
\par
%
Compared to \cite[Definition III.1]{wabersich22}, we adopt a more restrictive CBF decrease bound in~\cref{eq:cbf_decrease_bound}. Rather than requiring a decrease for some continuous function $\Delta h(x)$ only for $x\in \mathcal D \setminus \mathcal S$, we enforce~\cref{eq:cbf_condition} for all $x\in \mathcal D$ and impose a specific structure on $\Delta h$ as given in~\cref{eq:cbf_decrease_bound}. These additional requirements align \cref{def:barrier_function} more closely with the original definitions of CBFs for continuous-time systems~\cite{ames2016control} and their discrete-time counterparts~\cite{agrawal2017discrete}. As a result, \cref{def:barrier_function} enables systematic shaping of the closed-loop behavior by selecting an extended class~$\mathcal K$ function $\alpha$, which determines the set of feasible control inputs~\cref{eq:cbf_safe_control}. Illustrative examples of how different choices of $\alpha$ influence the closed-loop behavior can be found in~\cite{alan2023control}. The properties discussed above follow from \cite[Thm.~III.4]{wabersich22} and rely on the notion of control invariant sets.
%
\begin{definition}[Set Invariance]\label{def:control_invariant_set}
    A set $\Omega\subseteq\mathbb R^{n_x}$ is called control invariant for system~\cref{eq:linear_system} subject to~\cref{eq:constraints} if for all $x\in\Omega$ there exists an input $u\in\mathcal U$ such that $Ax + Bu\in\Omega$.
    The set is called invariant for autonomous systems if for all $x\in\Omega$ it holds that $Ax + B\pi(x)\in\Omega$ for some controller $\pi:\mathbb R^{n_x}\rightarrow\mathbb R^{n_u}$.
\end{definition}
%
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:cbf_properties}
    Let $\mathcal D\subset\mathbb R^{n_x}$ be a non-empty and compact set. Consider a CBF $h:\mathcal D\rightarrow\mathbb R$ with safe set $\mathcal S$ according to \cref{def:barrier_function}. If $\mathcal S \subset \mathcal D$ and $\mathcal D$ is an invariant set for system~\cref{eq:linear_system} under $u(k)=\kappa(x(k))$ for some $\kappa:\mathcal D\rightarrow\mathcal U$ with $\kappa(x)\in K_{\mathrm{CBF}}(x)$, then it holds that 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\mathcal S$ is an invariant set,
        \item $\mathcal S$ is asymptotically stable in $\mathcal D$.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
    The proof follows directly from the fact that~\cref{eq:cbf_condition} is more restrictive than the original definition \cite[Definition III.1]{wabersich22}.
\end{proof}
%
The properties established in \cref{def:barrier_function} and \cref{prop:cbf_properties} are fundamental for CBF-based safety filter design. Although constructing such CBFs is generally challenging, the next section demonstrates how set-based CBFs can be systematically designed to satisfy these requirements using invariant sets.
%
\section{Set-Based Control Barrier Functions}\label{sec:set_based_cbf}
This section presents a systematic approach for constructing a CBF based on a control invariant set $\Omega$. The resulting CBF satisfies Definition~\ref{def:barrier_function} and defines the safe set $\mathcal S=\Omega$. Specifically, for all $x\in\Omega$ and any $\alpha\in\mathcal K^e$, the CBF satisfies the first case in~\eqref{eq:cbf_decrease_bound}, establishing the invariance property in Proposition~\ref{prop:cbf_properties}, 1). Section~\ref{sec:robust_invariance_and_stability} extends this framework to guarantee asymptotic stability of the safe set by addressing the second case in~\eqref{eq:cbf_decrease_bound} according to Proposition~\ref{prop:cbf_properties}, 2).
%
\par
%
Consider a control invariant set $\Omega$, and note that scaling of the form $\gamma \Omega$ preserves invariance (see~\cref{lem:control_invariant_set_scaling}). For a given state $x$, we define
\begin{align}
    \gamma_{\Omega}(x)\triangleq \min_{\gamma\in \R, \gamma \geq 0}~\gamma~\mathrm{s.t.}~x\in\gamma\Omega. \label{eq:set_based_gamma}
\end{align}
The set-based CBF is given by
\begin{align}
    h(x) \triangleq 1 - \gamma_{\Omega}(x), \label{eq:set_based_cbf}
\end{align}
for which we can verify basic properties as detailed in the following as an intermediate result.
%
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:nominal_set_cbf}
    Let $\Omega$ be a convex control invariant set satisfying $0\in\mathrm{int}(\Omega) $ and $\Omega\subseteq \mathcal X$. The corresponding set-based CBF~\eqref{eq:set_based_cbf} satisfies \cref{def:barrier_function} for any compact $\mathcal D \supseteq \mathcal S$ with $\mathcal S = \Omega$, except for the second case in~\eqref{eq:cbf_decrease_bound}. Under application of $u(k)=\kappa(x(k))$ for some $\kappa:\mathcal D\rightarrow\mathcal U$ with $\kappa(x)\in K_{\mathrm{CBF}}(x)$, it holds that $\mathcal S$ is a forward invariant set.
\end{theorem}
%
The proof of \cref{thm:nominal_set_cbf} is provided in the appendix. It establishes that applying the CBF safety filter~\eqref{eq:cbf_filter} with the set-based CBF~\eqref{eq:set_based_cbf} constructed from a control invariant set $\Omega$ guarantees safety for all initial conditions $x(0)\in\Omega$, while permitting arbitrary choices of $\alpha\in\mathcal K^e$ to shape the decrease $\Delta h$. The impact of this design flexibility is demonstrated in the numerical simulations and experimental results presented in Sections~\ref{subsec:motion_control} and~\ref{sec:electrical_machine}.
%
\subsection{Robust Invariance ensures Asymptotic Stability of $\mathcal S$}\label{sec:robust_invariance_and_stability}
%
To ensure that the set-based CBF~\cref{eq:set_based_cbf} also satisfies the second case of~\cref{eq:cbf_decrease_bound}, and thus guarantees asymptotic stability of the safe set $\mathcal S$, we require the underlying set $\Omega$ to be robust control invariant with respect to an artificial disturbance set $\mathcal W$ containing the origin in its interior. Intuitively, this means that for every state in $\Omega$, there exists a control input that keeps the system within $\Omega$ despite any disturbance $w \in \mathcal W$. Although the actual system~\cref{eq:linear_system} is not affected by this auxiliary disturbance, incorporating $\mathcal W$ in the design introduces a margin of robustness. Consequently, a contracted set $\mathcal S = \nu\Omega$ with $\nu<1$ can be shown to be asymptotically stable under the set-based CBF, as formalized in the following.
%
\begin{definition}[Robust Control Invariant Set]\label{def:robust_control_invariant_set} 
    A set $\tilde\Omega\subseteq \mathcal \R^{n_x}$ is called robust control invariant for system~\cref{eq:linear_system} subject to~\cref{eq:constraints} with respect to an additive disturbance set $\mathcal W\subseteq\R^n$ if for all $x\in\tilde\Omega$ there exists an input $u\in\mathcal U$ such that $Ax + Bu + w\in\tilde\Omega$ for all $w\in\mathcal W$. The set is called robust invariant for autonomous systems if for all $x\in\tilde\Omega$ and $w\in\mathcal W$ it holds that $Ax+B\pi(x)+w\in\tilde \Omega$ for some controller $\pi:\mathbb R^{n_x}\rightarrow\mathbb R^{n_u}$.
\end{definition}
%
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:set_based_cbf}
    Let $\tilde\Omega\subset \R^{n_x}$ be a convex robust control invariant set with $0\in\mathrm{int}(\Omega)$ and $\mathcal S \subseteq \mathcal X$ for some disturbance $\mathcal W$ with $0\in\mathrm{int}(\mathcal W)$.
    Let $\Omega \triangleq \nu \tilde\Omega$ for some $\nu < 1$ such that $\tilde\Omega \ominus \mathcal W\subseteq \Omega$ holds.
    Then, the set-based CBF~\cref{eq:set_based_cbf} satisfies \cref{def:barrier_function} with safe set $\mathcal S=\Omega$ and domain $\mathcal D=\tilde \Omega$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    The missing part in addition to ~\cref{thm:nominal_set_cbf} is to show that the second case of the bound \cref{eq:cbf_decrease_bound} holds.
    A sufficient condition is to show that for all $x\in \mathcal D\setminus \mathcal S$ there exists an $u\in\mathcal U$ such that $h(Ax + Bu ) - h(x) \geq - h(x)$, which implies $h(Ax + Bu ) \geq 0$. Since $\mathcal D$ is a robust control invariant set, there exists an input $u\in\mathcal U$ such that $Ax+Bu+w\in\mathcal D$ for all $w\in\mathcal W$. By definition of the Pontryagin difference, it follows that $Ax+Bu\in\mathcal D\ominus \mathcal W$, which is a subset of $\mathcal S$ by assumption, completing the proof.
\end{proof}
%
The choice of $\mathcal W$ and $\nu$ reflects a trade-off between the size of the region from which the system can recover to the safe set and the conservativeness of the safety filter. Larger perturbations $\mathcal W$ allow for smaller values $\nu$, increasing the region of attraction $\tilde \Omega \setminus \nu\tilde\Omega$. This enables recovery from a broader set of unsafe states, but results in a smaller safe set $\mathcal S = \nu\Omega$ and thus a more restrictive safety filter. Conversely, if recovery from outside $\mathcal S$ is not required, robustness can be omitted and the nominal result of \cref{thm:nominal_set_cbf} applies.
%
A numerical simulation of \cref{thm:set_based_cbf} can be found in Section~\ref{subsec:mass_spring_damper}.
%
\section{Implementation and Design of Set-based \gls{CBF} Safety Filters}\label{sec:design_and_implementation}
%
A variety of methods exist for computing (robust) control invariant sets for linear systems, including classical set invariance~\cite{blanchini1999set}, viability theory~\cite{liniger2017real}, reachability analysis~\cite{gruber2023scalable}, and model predictive control design techniques~\cite{rawlings2017model}.
%
However, directly enforcing the \gls{CBF} constraint~\cref{eq:cbf_filter_decrease_condition} in a safety filter using a set-based CBF~\cref{eq:set_based_cbf} leads to a bi-level optimization problem. To address this, we introduce a sufficient reformulation that guarantees satisfaction of the \gls{CBF} condition~\cref{eq:cbf_filter_decrease_condition} through the following safety filter optimization problem:
%
\begin{subequations}
    \label{eq:set_based_cbf_filter}
    \begin{align}
        \min_{u\in\mathcal U, \gamma^+\geq 0} ~    & G(u, u_{\mathrm{des}}) \label{eq:set_based_cbf_filter_objective}\\
        \mathrm{s.t.} ~ & 1-\gamma^+ - h(x) \geq -\Delta h(x),\label{eq:set_based_cbf_filter_decrease_condition}\\
        & Ax + Bu \in \gamma^+ \Omega. \label{eq:set_based_cbf_filter_set_constraint}
    \end{align}
\end{subequations}
%
Here, the constraint~\cref{eq:set_based_cbf_filter_set_constraint} ensures that $1-\gamma^+$ serves as an upper bound on the set-based CBF, which is non-conservative since the optimizer can always select $\gamma^+$ and $u$ such that $1-\gamma^+ = h(Ax+Bu)$ holds. Consequently, the core challenge in designing a CBF safety filter of the form~\cref{eq:set_based_cbf_filter} reduces to formulating a suitable, and preferably convex, set-scaling constraint~\cref{eq:set_based_cbf_filter_set_constraint} for a given set $\Omega$. Sections~\ref{subsec:poly_h_rep}~-~\ref{subsec:predictive_safe_sets} illustrate this reformulation for several common classes of robust positive invariant sets.
%
Furthermore, Section~\ref{subsec:approximation_set_based_cbf} introduces learning-based approximations of set-based CBFs to reduce online computational complexity and Section~\ref{sec:stochastic} concludes with an extension towards stochastic uncertainties.
%
\begin{remark}
Evaluating $h(x) = 1 - \gamma_\Omega(x)$ in the set-based CBF safety filter decrease condition~\cref{eq:set_based_cbf_filter_decrease_condition} can require solving an optimization problem prior to each filter evaluation. This can pose challenges for real-time implementation, particularly in high-dimensional systems or when $\Omega$ is defined implicitly, such as by a convex hull or generator representation. A practical simplification is to augment the objective~\cref{eq:set_based_cbf_filter_objective} with a small penalty term $\rho {(\gamma^+)}^2$, which encourages the optimizer to select $\gamma^{+,*} = \gamma_\Omega(Ax+Bu)$, effectively yielding $h(Ax+Bu)$, which can be used directly at the next step. However, larger values of $\rho$ may introduce conservative interventions, and exact equivalence to~\cref{eq:set_based_cbf_filter} is only achieved as $\rho \rightarrow 0$.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Polytopes in H-Representation}\label{subsec:poly_h_rep}
    Most standard algorithms for computing (maximal) robust controlled invariant sets for linear systems subject to polytopic state and input constraints result in polytopes in half-space representation (H-representation) to define $\Omega$, see, e.g., \cite{herceg2013multi}. Such polytopes containing the origin can be expressed as:
    % \begin{align*}
        $\Omega = \{x\in\R^{n_x}~|~Hx\leq \onesVector\}$,
    % \end{align*}
    with $H\in\R^{N_h\times n_x}$. Since $\gamma \Omega = \{x \in\R^{n_x}~|~Hx \leq \gamma \onesVector\}$ it follows that
    \cref{eq:set_based_cbf_filter_set_constraint} can be efficiently implemented as
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:cbf_polytope_constraint}
        H(Ax + Bu) \leq \gamma^+ \onesVector.
    \end{equation}
\subsection{Data-Driven Safe Sets: Polytopes in V-Representation}
    Data-driven (robust) control invariant sets, such as those described in \cite{rosolia2021robust} for repetitive tasks or \cite{wabersich2021soft} for finite trajectories ending at the origin, are defined as polytopes in vertex representation (V-representation), that is,
    % \begin{align*}
        $\Omega = \left\{x=\sum_{i=0}^{N_v} \lambda _i v_i ~\middle|~ \sum_{i=0}^{N_v} \lambda_i = 1, \lambda_i\geq 0 \right\}$.
    % \end{align*}
    To ensure that $0\in\Omega$ we require $v_0=\{0\}$. From~\cite[Lemma 2]{wabersich2021soft} it follows that
    \begin{align*}
        \gamma_{\Omega}(x) = \min_{\{\lambda_i\}_{i=0}^{N_v}} 1-\lambda_0 ~\mathrm{s.t.}~ x = \sum_{i=0}^{N_v} \lambda_i v_i, \sum_{i=0}^{N_v} \lambda_i = 1, \lambda_i\geq 0.
    \end{align*}
    This allows to implement constraint~\cref{eq:set_based_cbf_filter_set_constraint} with $\lambda_i\geq 0$ as
    \begin{subequations}
        \begin{align*}
            Ax + Bu  = \sum_{i=0}^{N_v} \lambda_i v_i,~~
                \sum_{i=0}^{N_v} \lambda_i = 1,~~
                1-\lambda_0  = \gamma^+.
        \end{align*}
    \end{subequations}
\subsection{Scalable Safe Set Computations: Zonotopes}\label{subsec:zonotopic_set_based_cbf}
    Zonotopes are a special case of polytopes and provide preferable scalability properties for high-dimensional systems.
    Zonotopes are centrally symmetric and defined as 
    \begin{align*}
        \Omega = \left\{x = c + G \lambda ~\middle|~ \Vert \lambda \Vert_\infty \leq 1 \right\} ,
    \end{align*}
    where $c \in \R^{n_x}$ is the center and $G \in \R^{n_x \times N_g}$ is the generator matrix having $N_g$ generators.
    Based on the definition of zonotopes, it follows that \cref{eq:set_based_gamma} is equivalent to
    \begin{align*}
        \gamma_\Omega(x) = \min_{\gamma\in \R, \gamma \geq 0}~\gamma ~\mathrm{s.t.}~ x = \gamma c + G \lambda, \Vert \lambda \Vert_\infty \leq \gamma ;
    \end{align*}
 %   \begin{proof}
 %       By definition, it follows that
 %       \begin{align*}
 %           \gamma \Omega
 %           &= \left\{x = \gamma (c + G \lambda) ~\middle|~ \Vert \lambda \Vert_\infty \leq 1 \right\} \\
 %           &= \left\{x = \gamma c + G \gamma \lambda ~\middle|~ \Vert \lambda \Vert_\infty \leq 1 \right\} \\
 %           &= \left\{x = \gamma c + G \tilde{\lambda} ~\middle|~ \tilde{\lambda} = \gamma \lambda, \Vert \lambda \Vert_\infty \leq 1 \right\} \\
 %           &= \left\{x = \gamma c + G \tilde{\lambda} ~\middle|~ \tilde{\lambda} = \gamma \lambda, \Vert \gamma \lambda \Vert_\infty \leq \gamma \right\} \\
 %           &= \left\{x = \gamma c + G \tilde{\lambda} ~\middle|~ \Vert \tilde{\lambda} \Vert_\infty \leq \gamma \right\} ,
 %       \end{align*}
 %       where $\gamma \geq 0$ is assumed.
 %   \end{proof}
    which allows a reformulation of~\cref{eq:set_based_cbf_filter_set_constraint} as:
    \begin{subequations}
        \begin{align*}
            Ax + Bu = \gamma^+ c + G \lambda ,~
            \Vert \lambda \Vert_\infty  \leq \gamma^+,
        \end{align*}
    \end{subequations}
    with optimization variables $\lambda \in \R^{N_g},\gamma^+ \geq 0$.
\subsection{Predictive Safe Sets: Projected Invariant Sets}\label{subsec:predictive_safe_sets}
This section utilizes the fact that the feasible set of a robust predictive control problem is robust positive control invariant~\cite{rawlings2017model,wabersich2023} and can therefore be used to construct a set-based CBF. To this end, we show that a simple scaling of the predictive control constraints in state and input sequence space equivalently scales the corresponding set of feasible initial states.
%
\par
%
Consider the following robust predictive control problem, which is widely used in model predictive control~\cite{rawlings2017model} and in the context of predictive safety filters without CBF mechanisms~\cite{wabersich2023}:
\begin{subequations}
    \label{eq:psf_nominal}
    \begin{align}
        \min_{\{u_{i}\}_{i=0}^{N-1},\{x_{i}\}_{i=0}^{N}} \quad & G(u_0, u_{\mathrm{des}})
        \label{eq:psf_nominal_objective}                                                     \\
        \mathrm{s.t.}   \qquad\quad    & x_0=x,\label{eq:psf_nominal_initial_condition}\\
        \quad                 & x_{N} \in \gamma \mathcal X_f,             
        \label{eq:psf_nominal_terminal_constraint}                                           \\
                            & \text{for all}~i=0,..,N-1:                                   \\
        \quad                 & \quad x_{i+1} = Ax_{i} + Bu_{i},
        \label{eq:psf_nominal_dynamics}                                                      \\
        \quad                 & \quad x_{i} \in \gamma \mathcal X_i,
        \label{eq:psf_nominal_state_constraints}                                             \\
        \quad                 & \quad u_{i} \in \gamma \mathcal U_i.
        \label{eq:psf_nominal_input_constraints}
    \end{align}
\end{subequations}
%
For $\gamma=1$, this formulation recovers the standard robust predictive safety filter as in~\cite[Eq. (54) for $\mathcal E(x,u)=\mathcal W$]{wabersich2023}. At each time step, we solve~\eqref{eq:psf_nominal} for a given state $x$ and obtain an optimal input and state sequence $\{u_{i}^*\}$, $\{x_{i}^*\}$, from which $u(k) = u^*_{0}$ is applied to the system.
%
The objective~\cref{eq:psf_nominal_objective} approximates the desired safety filter objective~\cref{eq:cbf_filter_objective}, subject to the nominal state and input constraints~\cref{eq:psf_nominal_state_constraints} and~\cref{eq:psf_nominal_input_constraints}, along the predicted system dynamics~\cref{eq:psf_nominal_dynamics}.
%
To guarantee robust positive invariance under the optimal input sequence, the state and input constraints $\mathcal X_i$ and $\mathcal U_i$ are iteratively tightened along the prediction horizon, and the terminal set~$\mathcal X_f$ in~\cref{eq:psf_nominal_terminal_constraint} is chosen to be robust positively invariant, see, e.g.,~\cite[Sec.~15]{borrelli2017predictive}.
%
Under suitable choices it follows from~\cite[Thm.~15.1, 2.]{borrelli2017predictive} that the feasible set
\begin{align}
    \mathcal X_N^\gamma \triangleq \{x ~|~ \exists \{u_{i}\}_{i=0}^{N-1} \text{ s.t. } (x,\{u_{i}\}_{i=0}^{N-1}) \in \mathcal Z_N^\gamma\}.
    \label{eq:nominal_feasible_set_x}
\end{align}
with
\begin{align}
    \mathcal Z_N^\gamma \triangleq \{
    (x_{0},\{u_{i}\}_{i=0}^{N-1}) ~|~ 
    \text{\cref{eq:psf_nominal_terminal_constraint}-\cref{eq:psf_nominal_input_constraints}}\},
    \label{eq:nominal_feasible_set_z}
\end{align}
is a convex and compact robust control invariant according to \cref{def:robust_control_invariant_set}.
%
\par
%
To derive a set-based \gls{CBF} according to \cref{thm:set_based_cbf}, we need to find an efficient scaling operation of the feasible set~\cref{eq:nominal_feasible_set_x}, which is only defined implicitly via a projection of the high-dimensional set~\cref{eq:nominal_feasible_set_z} onto the set of feasible initial states~\cref{eq:nominal_feasible_set_x}. While this projection is generally intractable to compute, we can still implement~\cref{eq:set_based_cbf_filter_set_constraint} using \cref{eq:psf_nominal_terminal_constraint}-\cref{eq:psf_nominal_input_constraints} by scaling the set $\mathcal X_N^\gamma$ through $\gamma$ since $\gamma\mathcal X_N^1=\mathcal X_N^\gamma$, see \cref{lem:scaling_of_feasible_set}.
%
The CBF constraint~\cref{eq:set_based_cbf_filter_set_constraint} can therefore be enforced as follows:
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align*}
        x_0& = Ax+Bu, \\
        \quad                 & x_{N} \in \gamma^+ \mathcal X_f,\\
                            & \text{for all}~i=0,..,N-1: \\
        \quad                 & \quad x_{i+1} = Ax_{i} + Bu_{i},\\
        \quad                 & \quad x_{i} \in \gamma^+ \mathcal X_i,\\
        \quad                 & \quad u_{i} \in \gamma^+ \mathcal U_i.
\end{align*}
\end{subequations}
%
The resulting CBF-based safety filter~\cref{eq:set_based_cbf_filter} can thus be interpreted as a predictive safety filter~\cite{wabersich2023} augmented with a CBF mechanism, enabling systematic shaping of safety interventions near the boundary of the feasible safe set~\cref{eq:nominal_feasible_set_x}. Notably, this approach recovers the predictive CBF formulation in~\cite{wabersich22} for linear systems, providing a principled design procedure and eliminating the need for heuristic tuning of iterative constraint tightening or large soft constraint penalties.
%
\subsection{Approximate Set-based \gls{CBF}}\label{subsec:approximation_set_based_cbf}
%
To reduce the online computational burden of~\cref{eq:set_based_cbf_filter}, the set-based \gls{CBF} introduced can be efficiently approximated offline via imitation learning, following the approach in~\cite{didier2023approximate}. Specifically, the mapping $\gamma_{\Omega}(x)$ is approximated by a learned function $\bar \gamma_{\Omega}(x)$, trained on samples $\{x_j, \gamma_{\Omega}(x_j)\}$ generated offline, for example by uniform sampling within a relevant subset of $\mathcal D$. The resulting approximation $\bar h(x) = 1 - \bar \gamma_{\Omega}(x)$ enables direct implementation of~\cref{eq:cbf_filter} and the resulting safety filter is robust against small approximation errors, if $h(x)$ is a CBF according to Definition~\ref{def:barrier_function} \cite[Thm.~2]{didier2023approximate}.
