\subsection{Chain of Mass-Spring-Damper Systems}\label{subsec:mass_spring_damper}
%
% See: https://github.boschdevcloud.com/TOP105-SaVe/safe-by-design-toolbox/blob/paper/set-based-cbf/zonotope_based_cbf.py
To demonstrate the scalability of our approach, we use a chain of $5$~mass-spring-damper systems from \cite[Sec.~5]{conte2012}, i.e., $n_x = 10$ and $n_u = 5$.
Instead of discretizing the continuous-time system using the Euler method, as done in \cite{conte2012}, we use the exact discretization to obtain a more accurate discrete-time model.
To apply \cref{thm:set_based_cbf}, we choose the additive disturbance set $\mathcal W \subset \R^{10}$ as unit box and use the modeling framework CVXPY~\cite{diamond2016cvxpy} to solve the convex optimization problem.
By combining \cite{gruber2023scalable} and \cite{schafer2024}, the computation of a zonotopic under-approximation of the maximal robust control invariant set~$\mathcal{D}$ takes \SI{1}{\second}, where the set has 50~generators.
In contrast, computing the maximal (non-robust) control invariant set using \cite{herceg2013multi} is aborted prematurely after \SI{24}{\hour}.
%
\par
%
In \cref{fig:zonotope_based_cbf_sets}, we show the simulation results when choosing $\mathcal{S} = 0.9 \mathcal{D}$, $x_0 = \begin{bmatrix} -9 & 9 & 0 & \ldots & 0\end{bmatrix}^T \notin \mathcal{S}$, and sampling $u_{\mathrm{des}}(k) \in \R^5$ randomly from the input constraint set~$\mathcal U$.
In \cref{fig:zonotope_based_cbf_trajectories}, we present the evolution of the zonotope-based CBFs~$h$ for different $u_{\mathrm{des}}(k)$ randomly sampled from $\mathcal U$. The system always converges to the safe set, and once inside, the desired damping behavior is evident when approaching $h(x)=0$.
