\section{Experiment: Electric Motor}\label{sec:electrical_machine}
%
\begin{figure}
	\centering
	\includegraphics*[width=\linewidth]{figures/sim/tb_measurement_cbf_emachine.pdf}
	\caption{Test bench results of an electric machine with a change of the desired voltages at time $t = \SI{1}{\second}$ for different CBF decrease bound parameters $s\in[0.2,1]$ defining $\alpha(h(x))=s\cdot h(x)$ in~\eqref{eq:cbf_decrease_bound}. While the safety does not interfere with the desired input initially, a smaller bound results in an earlier intervention with a stronger `damping' effect near the safe boundary.}
	\label{fig:testbench_cbf_emachine}
\end{figure}
%
This section illustrates a real-world application of the set-based CBF safety filter designed using a maximal control invariant set (Theorem~\ref{thm:nominal_set_cbf}). To this end, we consider the safe control of an electric motor on a test bench, requiring high sampling rates.
%and display how the closed-loop behavior can be influenced by the choice of $\alpha(\Delta h(x))$.
%
The electric motor is a \gls{PMSM} which, at fixed speeds, can be described by a discrete-time LTI model of the form \eqref{eq:linear_system} with states $x = [i_{\mathrm{d}},\; i_{\mathrm{q}}]^\top \in \R^2$ and $u = [u_{\mathrm{d}},\; u_{\mathrm{q}}, 1]^\top \in \R^3$ where $i_{\mathrm{d}}$, $i_{\mathrm{q}}$, $u_{\mathrm{d}}$, $u_{\mathrm{q}}$ are the dq-components of the electric currents and voltages, respectively. The system is subject to polytope constraints on state and input $\mathcal{X} = \left\{x|H_{\mathrm{x}}x \leq h_{\mathrm{x}}\right\}$ and $\mathcal{U} = \left\{u|H_{\mathrm{u}}u \leq h_{\mathrm{u}}\right\}$, respectively. For more information on modeling the system and constraints, and potential applications of safety filters for \gls{PMSM}s, see \cite{rosch2023predictive}.
\par
The set-based CBF is constructed using the maximal positive control invariant set in H-representation~(\cref{subsec:poly_h_rep}), yielding the set-based CBF $h(x) = 1 - \gamma_{\Omega}(x)$ with $\gamma_{\Omega}(x)$ as in~\eqref{eq:cbf_polytope_constraint}. The CBF decrease bound is chosen as $\alpha(h(x)) = s \cdot h(x)$ for $s \in [0.2, 1]$ to illustrate its damping effect on closed-loop trajectories. The safety filter is implemented as a parametric quadratic program (QP) using~\eqref{eq:cbf_polytope_constraint}, with parameters $\theta = [x^\top,\; u_{\mathrm{des}}^\top,\; h(x)]^\top$, and solved explicitly via the MPT toolbox~\cite{herceg2013multi}, resulting in a piecewise affine control law with $127$ regions.
\par
On dSPACE1007 hardware, the average evaluation time is \SI{34.2}{\micro \second}, well below the system's sampling time of \SI{150}{\micro \second}. Experimental results (Fig.~\ref{fig:testbench_cbf_emachine}) show that smaller $s$ values yield earlier and more damped interventions near the safety boundary, while $s=1$ corresponds to a standard safety filter. This highlights the flexibility of the set-based CBF framework for tuning intervention behavior while ensuring real-time constraint satisfaction.
%


\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
This article introduced a unifying set-based control barrier function (CBF) framework for safety filter design in linear systems with convex constraints. By leveraging scalable invariant sets, including those defined implicitly or via predictive control, the proposed approach enables systematic and efficient construction of CBFs for high-dimensional and data-driven systems. The framework supports real-time implementation and learning-based approximations, while providing formal safety and stability guarantees. Numerical simulations on a high-dimensional mass-spring-damper system and a motion control task, along with experimental validation on an electric drive with strict timing requirements, demonstrate the method's effectiveness, flexibility, and practical applicability to safety-critical control problems.

