\section*{Introduction}

Measurement of scientific performance is very crucial for agencies regulating scientific enterprises with a common goal towards enhancing their scientific capacity and encouraging innovations. Contemporary science is very vast and evolving in the complex structure of diverse scientific fields and specializations, fostering interdisciplinary research \cite{van2004measuring, dworking2019emergent}. Traditionally, the performance of knowledge producers is measured along the volume -- providing a stable ranking for producers like countries \cite{braun1995scientometric}-- their citations serve as a critical metric reflecting the impact and influence of a piece of research within its scientific community \cite{moed2006citation}. Higher citation counts often indicate the recognition and utility of the work, making them a valuable proxy for assessing both individual and institutional contributions to scientific advancement. Moreover, citations provide a networked perspective on knowledge diffusion, capturing the dynamic relationships between articles, disciplines, and researchers \cite{radicchi2011citation, chen2010community, zhao2015analysis}. Citations are perceived as a proxy for gauging the impact of work; they suffer from biases caused mainly by varied practices across the disciplines and age of publication, among many other factors influencing citations \cite{van2004measuring, bornmann2008citation}. Therefore, it is recommended that the raw citation counts should be normalized before comparing performers from different fields \cite{waltman2015field, radicchi2012reverse, bornmann2020should, ke2023network}.  

%and therefore should be normalized \cite{bornmann2020should} to reduce the impact of the biases resulting in ranking the performers \cite{waltman2015field, radicchi2012reverse, ke2023network}. 

The number of publications can provide information on the productivity of a researcher, institution, or country; it is the quality of the publications that truly drives scientific progress, particularly in the context of breakthroughs and disruptive research \cite{park2023papers, ke2023network}. High-quality publications tend to challenge existing paradigms, introduce novel ideas, and open new avenues of inquiry, often leaving a profound impact on their respective fields \cite{ merton1973sociology}. Disruptive research, in particular, requires a focus on quality, as it redefines foundational knowledge and influences subsequent research trajectories \cite{ bird2014thomas}. The emphasis on quality over quantity ensures that resources are directed toward the promotion of impactful innovations rather than simply increasing production, which could dilute overall scientific progress \cite{chu2021slowed}. This perspective underscores the importance of identifying and promoting publications that demonstrate significant intellectual contributions and transformative potential. These publications can be identified as breakthrough research, which from the point of view of scientific innovation theory can be further categorized into {\it consolidating} and {\it disruptive} types, based on the properties of their local citation network \cite{park2023papers}. Here we propose to combine two indices, viz., the disruptive index (CD) \cite{park2023papers} and the breakthrough index \cite{ke2023network}, both derived from the local network statistics.  

In this study, we focus on countries performing in the domain of Physical Sciences, categorized in 10 fields and sub-categorized in 89 subfields. Their respective performance is measured by identifying the number of papers falling within the global domain bracket of the top $k$-percentile of the network-based normalized citations (NBNC) scores identified as breakthroughs \cite{ke2023network}. The underlying assumption of this approach differs from that of source-based normalization \cite{waltman2016review, chatterjee2016universality, radicchi2008universality, uzzi2013atypical}, as it does not presuppose uniform performance rates across the various subfields \cite{ke2023network}. As the theory of scientific [r]evolution that suggests that science advances with scientific breakthroughs that may emerge by reinforcing existing knowledge or from disruptive ideas \cite{kuhn1997structure, kuhn1970nature}, the breakthroughs can be categorized by the CD index: $\text{CD}>0$ implies that the breakthrough is disruptive or consolidating otherwise. This leads us to consider two performance indicators, viz., the number of breakthrough papers that are disruptive and the number of breakthrough papers that are consolidating. 

The macro enterprises, such as countries, may excel not in one, but in several research fields. The diversity in performance arguably facilitates a competitive environment, catalyzing the knowledge growth routed through the complexly intertwined knowledge network. Comparison of such producers then is not straightforward --- contemporary approaches undertake the aggregate performance scores along the metrics and rely on the ad-hoc ways of assigning weights to define a composite index for their performance mapping \cite{ioannidis2016multiple, naturalearth_physical, scimago_sjr}. Conceptually, these approaches do not entail the growing sub-structural complexities and therefore fail to capture the emerging capabilities of the performer that are routed through these. This situation is very analogous to what economists have already encountered when comparing the economic capabilities of countries, given the varied and intricate composition of their products. This is addressed by indexing the complexity of the products and the complexity of producers \cite{hidalgo2009building}, instead of comparing merely their economic performance, usually understood in terms of Gross Domestic Product. These indices provide one of the most advanced frameworks for evaluating economic capabilities, and their conceptual foundations can be systematically extended to facilitate comparative assessment of the quality of their research performance. Substituting subfields for products in the actual setting \cite{hidalgo2009building} we adopt an interpretation: {\it
"More complex countries perform in more complex subfields, and more complex subfields are those that can only be achieved by more complex countries."} 
The performance of countries in all subfields can be dealt with in a geometrical arrangement, which essentially constitutes a bipartite network in which countries and subfields are the two sets of nodes. This approach leverages the ranking of the country and the subfield using centrality measures (a generalization of the eigenvector centrality) derived from a bipartite network \cite{hidalgo2009building, tacchella2012new}. Centrality measures are calculated through the GENEPY scheme, which facilitates a ranking system that incorporates the complexity and diversity of scientific fields \cite{sciarra2020reconciling}. This method generates a ranking based on the competitive performances of the countries having crucial implications in science policy. Our study brings valuable insights needed to reshape policies that enhance the scientific capabilities and competence of macro enterprises.  


