Both NBNC and CD are local metrics that leverage the perspective of citing articles. These measures are computed from around $N_{P}=60$ million articles in the domain of Physical Sciences collected from OpenAlex, published during the years 1900 to 2023. The breakthroughs (BT) are selected with the top $5\%$ for each year. With this selection, an increasing profile in time is expected in the number of BTs, $N^{(BT)}_{s}(t)$, as a consequence of the law of exponential growth of science \cite{price1963little}. Considerable research has focused on using publication numbers to quantify the growth of science, where the doubling period is estimated to be 17.3 years \cite{bornmann2021growth}. Notice that the BT-selection criterion is applied year-wise for all articles, the increasing profile is seen for both the performance metrics in time $t$, viz. number of consolidating, $N^{(CN)}_{s}(t)$, and number of disruptive articles, $N^{(DI)}_{s}(t)$, in almost all subfields $s=1,...,.89$ (Fig. \ref{fig:timeseries_BT_DI}). The correlations between the $N^{(CN)}_{s}$'s and the $N^{(DI)}_{s}$'s are very weak. %Furthermore, for BT articles, the correlation between their NBNC scores and their being disruptive is notably weak \cite{baak2020new}??.   


