Analysis of the phase velocity trajectory, i.e. $N^{(CN)}_{s}(t) ~\text {vs} ~N^{(DI)}_{s}(t)$, facilitates further insight into the disruptive and consolidating phases of the underlying growth dynamics and helps identify growth dynamics patterns. We systematically approach this part. First, we notice that the phase trajectories of subfields are not on the same scale. We therefore use $\tilde{N}^{(CN/DI)}_{s}=N^{(CN/DI)}_{s}/{N}_{s}$ where ${N}_{s}$ is the total number of articles published in subfield $s$. Second, the phase trajectories of subfields are not all alike; therefore, we cluster subfields based on their phase dynamics ({\bf Materials and Methods}). This yields 12 clusters, 16 singletons, where the largest 4  clusters are of size $\ge 4$, covering around $26\%$ of subfields; see Fig. \ref{fig:clusters}(a). The largest cluster (cluster 1) includes {\it Astronomy and Astrophysics, Paleontology, Environmental Engineering}, and the other 6 subfields are from {\it Mathematics}. The average growth curve of this cluster begins with the disruptive phase in the early 1960s and transitions to the consolidating phase by the 1970s (Fig. \ref{fig:clusters}(b)). However, a minor but discernible turnaround is apparent in the decade of 2000. This reversal is more pronounced for cluster 2 during the 1990s. In contrast, subfields of clusters 3 and 4 entered the consolidating phase in the early decades. The other clusters exhibit different growth patterns (see {\it SI}, Fig. \ref{fig:ClusterDynamics}), so are the singletons ({\it SI}, Table \ref{tab:clustered_fields} enlists all the subfields appeared in clusters of size$\geq 2$.). In what follows, we drop the subfields classified as singletons that exhibit abrupt phase dynamics or appear less frequently in the phase plot ({\it SI}, Table \ref{tab:selected_subfields} enlists the selected subfields.). 

Next, we construct the country-subfield performance matrix, $\mathbf{C^{(CN/DI)}}$, with 61 subfields and 212 countries denoting the bipartite network constructed by arranging performance values in 7 decades (the last decade has only 4 years) of the countries in rows and the respective subfield in columns ({\bf Materials and Methods}). The filtered matrix $\mathbf{M}$ is the adjacency matrix derived from $\mathbf{C}$ using the Revealed Comparative Advantage (RCA) filtering ({\bf Materials and Methods}). As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:decade_panel}, performance is almost localized to a few countries in early time windows, spreading in emerging subfields and other countries in time after the  1980s. It becomes almost ubiquitous (geographically) in some of the subfields like {\it Global and Planetary Change, \it Geophysics,  \it Atmospheric Science} and others. Subfields such as  {\it Water Science and technological} exhibit as a very niche area in the early decades, which became ubiquitous in the later decades. As is evident, countries that perform only in a few subfields tend to perform in ubiquitous subfields. This triangulated structure is analogous to what is observed in Economics, suggesting thereby an inverse relationship between the ubiquity of the subfield and the fitness of the country  \cite{tacchella2012new}. This leads to a coupled self-consistent recurrence relation between fitness and ubiquity; Eq. 1 of the Ref. \cite{tacchella2012new}. That said, we apply GENEPY \cite{sciarra2020reconciling}, which offers a convergent solution for this self-consistent equation, while it also provides the ranking of countries and subfields based on their complexity scores ({\bf Materials and Methods}). 



