The decade-wise ranking of all performing countries is shown for disruptive BTs in the left panel and for consolidating BTs on the right of Fig. \ref{fig:Allcountry_ranks}. Increasing global contribution is evident in pushing the knowledge boundaries. These are further illustrated for the top 20 countries (top-ranked countries in the 1950s) in Fig. \ref{fig:country_evol_CN_DIs}. Great Britain, the USA, Germany, Japan, and Australia are in the top bracket of 5 countries in the first decade. The USA secures the 2 top ranks until the $5^{th}$ decade, scoring 7th rank in the $6^{th}$ decade. On the other hand, Israel, starting from the 6th position, holds a ranking in the top bracket after the 5th decade. Discerning fall is observed for Australia and Great Britain in the decade of 2000, but they bounce back in the last decade. Except for Germany and Ireland, all European countries that are ranked in the top 20 in the first decade lost their ranking in the $6^{th}$ decade, but excluding Switzerland and Austria, all rebounded in the last decade. Notice that the ranking of consolidating breakthroughs appears to be a bit more zigzag, because consolidation breakthroughs could be achieved while doing normal science \cite{kuhn1997structure}, leading to increased competition. However, the USA and Israel performed much more steadily than other countries. The $6^{th}$ decade, here as well, witnesses the downfall in the ranking of most of the European countries, and most of them rise in the last decade. 

The subfield rankings for the $5^{th}$, $6^{th}$, and $7^{th}$ decades are shown in Fig. \ref{fig:subfield_sankey_CN}. The complexity of the consolidating BTs in the $5^{th}$ decade is dominated by {\it Signal Processing, Computer Science Applications, Automated Engineering, Information Systems, and Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition}. In the $6^{th}$ decade, the niche is created by {\it Physical and Theoretical Chemistry, Surface Coating and Film, Signal Processing, Condensed Matter Physics, and Electronic, Optical and Magnetic Materials and Methods}. By the $6^{th}$ decade, subfields like {\it Numerical Analysis, Artificial Intelligence, Computer Science Applications, Information Systems}, and {\it Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition} had already become ubiquitous among the countries. It is worth mentioning here that this approach tends to rank a country performing in less ubiquitous subfields higher than a country that is also performing in more ubiquitous subfields; see {\it SI}, Fig. \ref{fig:Decadal_Subfield_Ranking} for other decades. 

