\section*{Discussion}

The citation-network-based approach is becoming increasingly popular in gauging the impact of research outcomes. However, different fields have different citation practices that are also evolving with scientific paradigms and technological advancements in communicating research outcomes. When writing an article, a scientist may focus on different aspects at different stages to refer to an article. Many researchers advocate for contemplating a pluralistic view of citations, acknowledging elements from various perspectives. From this standpoint, the NBNC supplies a paradigm for assessing the academic impact of an article offering a normalized performance metric, which is rationalized with respect to an average perspective of citing articles over time. NBNC has been reported to reduce citation biases, but it does not explicitly favor the egalitarian assumption about the performance of all disciplines \cite{ke2023network, radicchi2008universality, uzzi2013atypical, waltman2016review, vaccario2024quest, gates2024reply}. In its present form, one needs to consider a long time-horizon to capture the sleeping beauties that occur mainly in the subfields of physics, chemistry, and mathematics \cite{ke2015defining,glanzel2003better}. Therefore, for practical reasons, in this study we prefer to consider the time horizon $T=10$ as suggested in Refs. \cite{ke2023network,zeng2019increasing,sinatra2016quantifying}. We have considered the same time-horizon in computing the CD index that we utilized to detect whether a breakthrough article is consolidating or disruptive. This novel approach facilitates a framework to capture the phase-evolution dynamics of subfield growth. While other authors consider taxonomy-based indicators, relating scientific evolution with the Kuhn cycle \cite{park2023papers, packalen2015new}, our approach capitalizes on citations serving the purpose of the present study. Both metrics depend on the quality and quantity of the data. We considered OpenAlex citation data, which is reported to have one of the largest citation databases encapsulating most of the data from different databases \cite{culbert2025reference}, to compute these metrics. 

Our study facilitates a ranking of the countries based on their competitive performance, which is a crucial component for science policy, but largely missing in extant literature. The method developed in econometrics, viz. GENEPY turned out to be the key for competitive assessment; therefore, it is employed here to achieve this purpose. Considering that scientific breakthroughs are sporadic, only those subfields are considered in the competitive performance ranking that have consistent growth profiles. The scaling of performances in subfields by the total number of papers further controls the comparative advantage taken by low-performing countries. However, we do not control the performances resulting from the country collaborations here, which could be an important factor for countries performing in a few subfields with high complexity scores (See {\it SI}, Fig. \ref{fig:MatrixplotCN} \& Fig. \ref{fig:MatrixplotDI} for example). 

The main finding of our study is that the consistent competitive performances are exhibited by the USA and Israel, and the comebacks by the European countries. Moreover, we find a nonlinear relationship (power law) between the GERD and breakthrough performances. The relationship between GERD and complexity ranking is observed to be highly nonlinear, with some outliers. Understanding the relationship between competitive rankings and academic freedom in research  \cite{spannagel2023academic}, competitive research environment, norms and ethical standards in research publication\cite{pitak2012morality}, and successful collaborations \cite{bammer2008enhancing} could be a further course of research. This might bring out systematic, focused, and strategic policy directives for science policy for the country's competitive performance.



\section*{Materials and Methods}



\subsection*{Data}
The data is extracted from the OpenAlex of around 60 million articles published from $1900$ to $2023$ in the Physical Sciences domain encompassing 10 fields, viz. Engineering, Computer Science, Environmental Science, Physics $\&$ Astronomy, Material Science, Chemistry, Earth and Planetary Science, Mathematics, Energy, and Chemical Engineering. The $NBNC$ score and the $CD$ index were computed for every paper, and their citations were also computed from the corpus of such papers. This dataset was then used to analyze publications from $1950$ to $2013$.However, the calculation of the $NBNC$ score and the $CD$ index computed from these papers was performed using the complete dataset.
%Using this corpus, we conduct analysis of papers published from $1950$ to $2013$, while forward and backward citation, we used complete corpus.  %This results .... citations at our disposal.  
% The $NBNC$ score and the $CD$ index were computed for papers having a DOI and their citations from the corpus of such papers.

\subsection*{NBNC scores and CD index}
$NBNC_{f}$ is computed by evaluating the annual averages of the citations of the co-cited publications for normalizing the citations, $c^{(f)}_{t}$, in year $t$ of the focal paper, $f$, and finally taking the cumulative sum till time horizon $T$ after its publication\cite{ke2023network}:
\begin{eqnarray}\label{NBNC}
 \text{NBNC}_{f}= \sum_{t=0}^{T} \hat{c}_{t},
 ~~~\text{where}~~~
  \hat{c}^{(f)}_{t}=N_{f;t} \frac{c^{(f)}_{t}}{\sum_{j=1}^{N_{f;t}}\gamma^{t}(\text{cocited}^{(f)}_{j;t})},
\end{eqnarray}
$c^{(p)}_{t}=\gamma^{t}(p)$ is citation count after $t$ years of publication, $\text{cocited}^{(p)}_{t}$ is the set of co-cited papers, $\text{cocited}^{(p)}_{j;t}$ is the $j$'th element $\text{cocited}^{(p)}_{t}$, $N_{p;t}$ is the number of papers co-cited with $p$ at time $t$, or the cardinality of $\text{cocited}^{(p)}_{t}$, and $T$ is the time-horizon. In the analysis, we have chosen $T=10$ and recognized publications in top $5\%$ NBNC score in the same year as the breakthrough papers of the year, $n_{BT}(T)$. The breakthroughs are further categorized into subfields using the sub-field labels provided by OpenAlex, yielding $n^{(s)}_{BT}(T)$ BTs in $s$-subfield in year $T$. For CD, On the other hand, the basic idea is to distribute the citation count of the focal paper, $C^{(f)}$, into two, viz. the number of publications citing the paper without its references, $C^{(f)}_{x;T}$, and with references, $C^{(f)}_{y;T}$; $C^{(f)}_{T}=C^{(f)}_{x;T}+C^{(f)}_{y;T}$. Defining $C^{R^{f}}_{T}$ as the total number of citations earned by references of $f$, excluding those citing $f$ and the citation by $f$ \cite{wu2019large}, the $CD_{T}$ may be computed as
\begin{equation}
    CD_{T}=
    \frac{C^{(f)}_{x;T}-C^{(f)}_{y;T}}{C^{(f)}_{T}+C^{R^{f}}_{T}}.
\end{equation}
It can be readily interpreted that $C^{(f)}_{T}=\sum_{t}^{T} c^{(f)}_{t}$. Notice that the numerator enables $CD$ to act as a filter, categorizing impact of the publications in {\it disruptive}, $CD>0$, and {\it consolidating}, $CD<0$. For example, in the $s$-subfield $n^{(s)}_{BT}(T)=n_{CN}^{(s)}(T)+n_{DI}^{(s)}(T)$ in year $T$. 

\subsection*{The Country-Subfield Bi-partite Network}
\subsubsection*{Performance Matrices}
We consider performances $n^{(DI)}_{c,s}(t)$ and $n^{(CN)}_{c,s}(t)$ of the country $c$ in subfield $s$ at time $t$ such that $\sum_{c}n^{(CN/DI)}_{c,s}(t)=\tilde{N}^{(CN/DI)}_{s}(t)$. Let $\mathbf{DI}_{c,s}(t)=n^{(DI)}_{c,s}(t)$ and $\mathbf{CN}_{c,s}(t)= n^{CN}_{c,s}(t)$ be the performance matrices, representing performances along the disruption and consolidation breakthroughs respectively, where rows represent the countries and columns denote the subfields. We use $Y=|X|_{Bin,R^{*}}$ where
\begin{equation}
\label{mod}
    Y_{cs}=\begin{cases} 1, &  \text{if}~~ X_{cs}>R^{*}\\
                         0, &  \text{if}~~ X_{cs}<R^{*}.
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}
\subsubsection*{RCA filtering}
The RCA filtered matrix $\mathbf{M}^{(X)}=|\mathbf{RCA}_{X}|_{Bin,R^{*}}$ is a semi-positive binary matrix, where
\begin{equation}
\label{RCA}
    RCA_{X}=\frac{X_{cs}}{\sum_{s'}X_{cs'}}/\frac{\sum_{c'}X_{c's}}{\sum_{c',s'}X_{c's'}}.
\end{equation}
Following \cite{balassa1965trade} we consider $R^{*}=1$. 
\subsubsection*{Adjacency Matrix}
Using definitions (\ref{RCA}, \ref{mod}), we introduce binary matrices that strictly define the country-subfield bi-partite network
\begin{eqnarray}
 \mathbf{M}^{(DI)}&=&|\mathbf{RCA}_{DI}|_{Bin,1}, \\
 \mathbf{M}^{(CN)}&=&|\mathbf{RCA}_{CN}|_{Bin,1}.
\end{eqnarray}

\subsection*{Cluster Identification}
We determine the temporal similarity between the time series of the dynamics of subfields for that we used dynamic time wrapping(DTW)\cite{berndt1994using}.
Followed the DTW distance of all time windows for every subfield. We transformed the distance matrix into a similarity matrix using a Gaussian kernel, defined as :
\begin{equation}
\label{Gaussian}
    DT(i,j)=\exp\left(-\frac{D(i,j)^2}{2\sigma^2}\right);
\end{equation}
where \( D(i,j) \) represents the DTW distance between time series \( i \) and \( j \), and \( \sigma \) is a standard deviation.

\noindent The similarity metric, \( DT(i,j) \), is used to cluster the subfields, for which we deployed the Leiden algorithm\cite{traag2019louvain}.

