\subsection*{The GENEPY Method}
We use the framework of Sciarra et al.\cite{sciarra2020reconciling}. The goal of this framework (as well as its predecessors) is to obtain two sets of related scores for both the countries ($D_{c;t}$) and the subfields ($C_{s;t}$), where these scores are related to each other by an iterative relationship. For this study, we adopt an interpretation analogous to economic complexity-ubiquity \cite{hidalgo2009building}: 

Following \cite{tacchella2012new} this interpretation may be translated into self-consistent coupled equations of $D_{c}(t)$ and $C_{s}(t)$ (Eqs. (1) of \cite{tacchella2012new}). Ref. \cite{sciarra2020reconciling} suggested that these coupled equations may well be solved by linearizing the system of equations, which results in an approximate solution constituting an eigen-problem. Under this approximation, $D_{c;t}$ and $C_{s;t}$ may be interpreted as eigenvectors of a similarity matrix.    

Let $k_{c;t}$ and $k'_{s;t}$ s are the column-wise and an adjusted row-wise sum of $\mathbf{C}_{t}$, defined as
\begin{equation}
    k_{c;t}=\sum_{s}M_{cs;t},\,\, \, k'_{s;t}=\sum_{c}M_{cs;t}/k_{c;t}.
\end{equation}
Traditional methods ignore details embedded in these geometric arrangements; the performance ranking is computed from the degree centrality only. On the other hand, deviations would be seen as the diversity and complexity become more effective, which may well be captured in the multi-component centrality of the proximity matrix \cite{sciarra2018change}. As the analogy follows, we compute a rationalized matrix $\mathbf{A}_{t}$ as
\begin{equation}
    A_{c,s;t}=\frac{M_{c,s;t}}{k_{c;t}k'_{s;t}},
\end{equation}
and evaluate $D_{c;t}$ as principal eigenvector of a symmetric matrix $\mathbf{U=AA}'$ for ranking the countries, and $C_{s;t}$ as the principal eigenvector of $\mathbf{V=A'A}$ for ranking subfields.
As $\mathbf{U}$ and $\mathbf{V}$, both are proximity matrices, the diagonals may be assigned to the same constant value; following \cite{sciarra2020reconciling}, for both matrices all the diagonal entries are set to $0$. The GENEPY-indices may be evaluated using the eigenvectors corresponding to the $r$ largest eigenvalues of the matrices. We consider $r=2$ to obtain a convergent result. 

