\section{Redshift} \label{Redshift}

\noindent In the previous sections, we have computed the quasinormal modes for the massless generalised Bronnikov--Ellis wormhole. However, if the geometry under study is surrounded by a dark matter halo or any generic matter distribution, the QNMs calculated for the isolated compact object may differ from the observed values, as the environment may affect the generation of GW as well as their propagation. Such environments cause the spectrum to be red-shifted as documented in \cite{Barausse2014Can, pezzella2024quasinormal, Liu2025} for the black hole spacetime embedded in a halo. 

\noindent In the case of the Bronnikov--Ellis wormhole, similar redshifts in the fundamental mode can be found, if one considers the wormhole with non-zero ADM mass, as 
noted by the authors of \cite{blazquez2018scalar}. We will first find the QNMs (fundamental and overtones) for the $M\neq0$ case in the $n=2$ Bronnikov--Ellis geometry. Thereafter, in our second example, we will
find the QNMs for a
modified  $n=2$ geometry with a new redshift function 
having an extra parameter $M$ \cite{alfaro2024quasinormal}. 
In these two scenarios, using the QNMs obtained, we then
verify if we have a parameter-controlled redshift, similar to
the environment-induced redshift mentioned above. The modelling of such red-shifts is crucial as the red-shifted modes may appear to be QNMs of the original isolated geometry with a larger throat radius (as $\omega$ varies inversely with $b_0$). 

\noindent Let us first fix our general idea using some well-studied potentials as toy examples. We will
try to find out how the $\omega_{QNM}$ vary if we change the width 
or other parameters present in two well-known potentials which have been
investigated and used extensively in the past.

\subsection{Rectangular Barrier:}
\noindent The rectangular barrier has the form:
\begin{equation}
V(x) =
\begin{cases}
V_0 & \text{for } 0 \leq x \leq a, \\
0 & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
The exact transmission amplitude is $a_t = 4\omega k e^{-i \omega a}((\omega + k)^2e^{-i k a}-(\omega - k)^2e^{i k a})^{-1}$, where $k=\sqrt{\omega^2-V_0}$.

\noindent In order to obtain the quasi-normal spectra, we need purely outgoing waves, i.e. $a_t \rightarrow \infty$, \cite{boonserm2011quasi} which implies $(\omega + k)^2e^{-i k a}-(\omega - k)^2e^{i k a}=0$, and we can solve this equation numerically to get the QNMs of the rectangular barrier.

\noindent Here, we are interested in how the QNM spectra change if we change the width (a) of the potential while keeping the height of the barrier fixed (say $V_0=1$).

