\begin{frontmatter}
\begin{abstract}
%This study thoroughly examines the ethical considerations of incorporating artificial intelligence (AI) in medical imaging, from data collection to deployment. Using an analytical methodology, we evaluate ethical implications across five stages: data collection, data processing, model training, model evaluation, and deployment. Each stage is scrutinized for compliance with essential ethical principles: data privacy and security, bias and fairness, transparency and explainability, accountability, and consent and autonomy. Data collection involves obtaining patient consent and anonymizing information for privacy. Model training addresses potential biases. Evaluation ensures AI systems are transparent, fair, and reliable, enabling healthcare professionals to integrate them into clinical decisions. Deployment emphasizes ongoing ethical assessment in real-world medical settings. We analyze how ethical considerations and accessibility issues affect private, public, and neutral third parties. Finally, we propose tailored inquiries for each AI development stage, outlining principles for creating ethically robust AI systems that align with patient care and data control criteria.
\textbf{Context:}  
The integration of artificial intelligence (AI) in medical imaging raises crucial ethical concerns at every stage of its development, from data collection to deployment. Addressing these concerns is essential for ensuring that AI systems are developed and implemented in a manner that respects patient rights and promotes fairness.\\
\textbf{Objectives:}  
This study aims to explore the ethical implications of AI in medical imaging, focusing on five key stages: data collection, data processing, model training, model evaluation, and deployment. The goal is to evaluate how these stages adhere to fundamental ethical principles, including data privacy, fairness, transparency, accountability, and autonomy.\\
\textbf{Methods:}  
An analytical approach was employed to examine the ethical challenges associated with each stage of AI development. We reviewed existing literature, guidelines, and regulations concerning AI ethics in healthcare and identified critical ethical issues at each stage. The study outlines specific inquiries and principles for each phase of AI development.\\
\textbf{Results:}  
The findings highlight key ethical issues: ensuring patient consent and anonymization during data collection, addressing biases in model training, ensuring transparency and fairness during model evaluation, and the importance of continuous ethical assessments during deployment. The analysis also emphasizes the impact of accessibility issues on different stakeholders, including private, public, and third-party entities.\\
\textbf{Conclusion:}  
