	\section{Conclusion}
	\label{conclu}

	This paper addresses the problem of estimating the covariance matrix for positively correlated assets. Empirical evidence from Ken-French factor-sorted portfolios confirms the presence of strong positive correlations and motivates our research. The proposed ERSE estimator enforces a minimum deviation from the uniform vector and uses a PER procedure to adjust paired eigenvectors while preserving orthogonality. Theoretically, we show that the leading eigenvector departs more from the null space than the others, and that modestly amplifying weaker factors improves the covariance matrix estimation. The PER steps can be viewed as sequential linear shrinkage on eigenvalues, boosting smaller values and contracting larger ones. We also demonstrate that ERSE remains computationally feasible even in high dimensions. Out-of-sample tests across multiple Ken-French datasets reveal that ERSE yields lower portfolio variance than benchmark estimators. Further checks show that ERSE produces covariance matrices with smaller condition numbers, more concentrated and stable portfolio weights, and consistent gains in every subperiod and under different estimation windows.

	There are some potential directions for future research. First, extend ERSE to mixed-correlation settings. Second, relax the multivariate normality assumption by deriving shrinkage formulas for nonparametric return distributions. Third, leverage machine-learning techniques on high-dimensional financial data, such as macroeconomic indicators, factor returns, order-book metrics, to learn adaptive shrinkage coefficients and improve ERSE’s responsiveness to changing market conditions.

	%This paper addresses the problem of covariance matrix estimation for positively correlated assets. Empirical results based on Ken-French factor-sorted portfolios confirm the presence of strong and persistent positive correlations, underscoring the relevance of our approach. We propose a novel covariance matrix estimator for positively correlated assets, called the Eigenvector Rotation Shrinkage Estimator (ERSE). Our theoretical analysis demonstrates that the dominant eigenvector exhibits a more significant deviation from the null space than the other eigenvectors, and moderately increasing the deviation of weak factors within the rotation-equivariant framework improves the covariance matrix estimator. The ERSE approach imposes a constraint on the eigenvectors to ensure that their deviation from the uniform vector exceeds a specified threshold. We introduce a Paired Eigenvector Rotation (PER) technique, which iteratively reduces the deviation between paired eigenvectors while maintaining their orthogonality. We show that the ERSE method can be interpreted as a series of linear shrinkage applications to the eigenvalues, as the PER technique amplifies weak eigenvalues while reducing strong ones. Additionally, we demonstrate that the computational cost of the ERSE method remains manageable for high-dimensional assets. The results show that the ERSE method outperforms other strategies in terms of risk management for positively correlated assets, achieving the lowest out-of-sample variance across most datasets. Further robustness checks provide strong evidence of the effectiveness of the proposed ERSE method. First, the ERSE method consistently produces a covariance matrix estimator with lower condition numbers, making it a reliable tool for risk management and portfolio optimization. Second, the ERSE strategy generates more concentrated and stable portfolio weights with narrower ranges and fewer extreme values, indicating the robustness of the constructed portfolio. Third, a subperiod analysis confirms that the ERSE strategy consistently outperforms its competitors in all subperiods, highlighting its robustness across different time intervals. Fourth, in robustness tests with different estimation windows, the ERSE strategy consistently outperforms others and is less sensitive to sample size variations. Finally, robustness tests using randomly sampled assets from the 650MD dataset show that the ERSE strategy outperforms its competitors in reducing out-of-sample risk.

