We consider two different regimes of flow networks, networks where the length of the edges are adjusted, and networks where the radius of the flow networks are adjusted. Networks that adapt by adjusting the radius of the flow network have been studied previously, including for \textit{physarum}. These dynamics are best understood for systems that are not exploring a space but already have a dense and connected network that includes loops. Therefore in these systems we can always have sources or sinks connected to the network within a loop or via a ancillary node (analogous to the ground node of electrical circuits) which is removed when identifying a reduced cycle or loop matrix. 

In the case of a flow network, \textit{physarum}, it is possible to examine the driven dynamics of these systems with changing edge length, this can reproduce behavior such as exploring a space, or maze, and moving the entire flow network. In this case, we need to have edges wherein $g_\text{min}\rightarrow 0$ for edges linking sources or sinks into a loop. In the case of these networks we use edges with zero conductivity to connect a branching tree like structure. These include edges that connect sources and sinks to the network as part of a larger loop.  \textcolor{red}{Not now convinced this is any different than resistor networks, review notes, I think part of the issue is how does $L_{ij}$ change under this bias, or if not change, how do I account for moving nodes throughout the network but attempt to keep the length of the loop edges constant, taking them to infinity kind of makes sense.} 
