\begin{prop}
    If $f\in \mathcal B_{\mu}$ for some $\mu\in (0,1]$ and $\lambda>0$ then $f^{\lambda}\in \mathcal B_{\mu([\lambda]+1)}$.
\end{prop}
{\it Proof.} We have
$$
\frac{\left(f(x)^\lambda\right)'}{x^{\mu([\lambda]+1)-1}}=\lambda\left(\frac{f(x)}{x^{\mu}}\right)^{[\lambda]}\frac{f'(x)}{x^{\mu-1}}\, f(x)^{\lambda-1-[\lambda]},
$$
which is a product of completely monotonic functions, since $f\in \mathcal B_1$ and the exponent $\lambda-1-[\lambda]$ is negative.\hfill $\square$
